#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Parallel-session helper for this repo. Standard: .agents/SESSIONS.md. Stdlib only.

  session start <task> [--own PATH]... [--provider P] [--objective TEXT] [--robot] [--batch ID] [--here]
  session capacity [--lanes N] [--json] [--here]
  session status [--json] [--all] [--here]
  session resume <task|branch|ref> [--as NAME] [--own PATH]... [--provider P] [--objective TEXT] [--here]
  session land [--title T] [--pr] [--no-merge] [--local] [--kf11-verdict yes|no --kf11-reviewer NAME]
               [--owners-decided TEXT] [--here]
  session finish [--task TASK] [--here]
  session retire [--reason TEXT] [--task TASK] [--dry-run] [--here]
  session wip-backup [--here]          (run by .agents/hooks/post-commit, in the background)

Managed by org-design-tooling/repo-kit; overwritten on every kit update.
The repository is the one this script lives in (the parent of .agents/), not the one the shell happens
to be in (kit 17): a lane that ran one repo's script from another repo's folder used to cut its worktree
in the wrong repo, silently. From a shell in a different repository every command refuses in one line
naming both, exits 2 and changes nothing; --here says the shell's repository is the one meant. From a
shell in no repository the script's own repository is used. Inside the script's repository the shell's
own worktree is the one acted on, so the primary's copy of the script can drive any session worktree.
Each run reports one event to Witness when Witness is on the machine, fail-open (WS-DDR-150, kit 17).
`start` and `land` turn on the pre-push portability check for this clone (activate_prepush);
`land` runs the same check itself before it pushes.
Before it pushes, `land` also runs the repository's own pre-land commands, declared under `pre_land` in
.agents/repository-policy.json, on the rebased tree; one that fails stops the land (kit 18).
`land` lands locally by default (squash onto origin/<base>, fast-forward push, no GitHub run);
it opens a PR only for ask paths, a base branch whose ruleset or protection requires a PR or
status checks, or `--pr` (WS-DDR-143 amendment 2026-09-24).
After a landing the session branch follows its squash, and a later `land` from the same worktree
skips any commits that already landed, so it never replays them onto their own squash (kit 12).
A file the branch untracks (`git rm --cached`) stays on disk through land's rebase: it is copied
into the worktree's git dir first and put back after, and `finish` waits while a copy is left (kit 13).
A read of a worktree, above all another session's, runs with GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0 (READ_ONLY): a plain
`git status` takes the worktree's index.lock while it walks it, and the other session's `git add` in that
moment fails (kit 20).
`start` refuses to cut a worktree that would leave the disk under SESSION_MIN_FREE_GB (10) free, and warns under
SESSION_WARN_FREE_GB (25); SESSION_ALLOW_LOW_DISK=1 starts anyway, once. Fail-open on any measuring error (kit 21).
`land` measures content changes against KF-11 (over 25 files per session or per agent per day lands only as a
reviewed diff): advisory, with a review summary in the handoff; SESSION_KF11_ENFORCE=1 makes it a refusal (kit 23).
Kit 34: only prose and data files count (markdown, text, YAML, JSON, CSV; not code, and not JSON or YAML under a
fixtures folder or stamped by its generator), and the review block carries a verdict line that
`land --kf11-verdict yes|no --kf11-reviewer NAME` fills with who and when; a no refuses the land.
`commit-guard`, run by .agents/hooks/pre-commit, refuses a commit on the primary checkout and, in a worktree `start`
pinned, a commit on another branch or from another session; `status` names paths staged on the primary (kit 28).
Kit 29: after every commit on an agent/* or claude/* branch, .agents/hooks/post-commit runs `wip-backup` in the
background, which pushes the branch tip to origin as refs/wip/<branch> (a backup, never a landing: no workflow runs,
no branch appears); `land`, `finish` and `retire` delete it. `retire` keeps abandoned or superseded work on a pushed
archive tag with a ledger row (retirement.py, shared with the worktree reaper) and removes the worktree.
Kit 33: `land` derives a signal stub from the session's handoff. When the handoff's "Decisions made" or "Unresolved
risks or decisions" field is filled and the change touches no signal file, `land` writes .intent/signals/
SIG-<repo>-<task>-<date>.md (the fields' lines verbatim, the commit range, `derived_by: session land`), creates the
folder and its CONTEXT.md when absent, and lands it with the work. Robot sessions, empty or "none" fields and a
change that already carries a signal are skipped; a failure to write it never blocks the land (reported to Witness).
Kit 35 closes three work-loss paths found by the 2026-11-10 check of the kit's implicit states. `finish` keeps ignored
files the way `retire` does before `git worktree remove`, which deleted them without asking and exited 0 (moved to the
primary, the newer copy winning, the older archived; regenerable ones go with the worktree; one it cannot keep stops the
finish with nothing removed). `wip-backup` leaves its result in the worktree's git dir, and `status` shows each
session's backup (current, behind, failing or none, with its age) and says when backups are off in the shell. `status`
names a land stopped at a rebase conflict by its branch; the hourly reaper reports it as stuck and backs it up under the
branch's own name too.
Kit 39, two people in one repository: a backup push carries a lease and is refused when the remote ref holds another
clone's commit; land, finish, retire and the reaper remove a backup only when its tip is this clone's work; `status`
reads the remote ref and says "not checked" rather than "current" when it cannot; `start` refuses a task name another
clone holds on origin; an optional person slug (`git config session.person`, SESSION_PERSON) goes into every name;
a combined landing adds a Co-authored-by trailer for each other author (SESSIONS.md, kit 39).
Kit 40: `start` claims the new branch's name on the remote (refs/claims/<branch>, a create-only push), so two clones
that start one name before either has saved cannot both go on, and a start that dies after its claim is taken up again
by its own clone; `status --all` lists every claimed or backed-up task on the remote with its holder, age and whether
it is ahead of the base branch; `resume` continues another clone's unlanded task as a new attempt that cites it and
never changes it; a land that conflicts with another person's landing is refused, names that landing, leaves the task
record blocked and not complete, and waits for `land --owners-decided TEXT` (SESSIONS.md, kit 40).
Kit 41, batch lanes: `start --batch ID` marks a lane a batch cuts for parallel width. It is cut only while the disk
stays above the batch line (SESSION_BATCH_FREE_GB, default the 25 GB warning line) after this checkout, measured with
each blob rounded up to a 4 KiB block; otherwise it is refused with exit 75, nothing created, which means queue it and
start it once a running lane has landed and `finish` freed its checkout. SESSION_ALLOW_LOW_DISK never admits one. The
room between the batch line and the floor is kept for the worktrees that land work. `capacity --lanes N` says how many
of N lanes fit now (exit 0 when all do, 75 when some must queue; read-only).
"""
import argparse, contextlib, datetime as dt, filecmp, fnmatch, importlib.util, io, json, os, re, shutil, signal, subprocess, sys, time
from pathlib import Path

SLUG = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]{0,80}$")
FOOTER = "\n\n\U0001F916 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)"
WRONG_REPO = 2  # exit status of a refusal: the shell is standing in a repository this script does not belong to


def _kit_dir():
    """The checkout this script is installed in: <checkout>/.agents/bin/session. None for a copy anywhere else."""
    p = Path(__file__).resolve()
    return p.parents[2] if p.parent.name == "bin" and p.parent.parent.name == ".agents" and len(p.parts) > 3 else None


KIT_DIR = _kit_dir()


def _kit_version():
    try:
        return (KIT_DIR / ".agents" / "KIT_VERSION").read_text().strip()
    except Exception:
        return "unknown"


KIT_VERSION = _kit_version()  # read once: `finish` removes the worktree this script lives in before the event is sent


def run(args, cwd, check=True, env=None):
    """env: variables added to this process's environment for this one call (READ_ONLY for a read)."""
    r = subprocess.run(args, cwd=cwd, text=True, capture_output=True, env={**os.environ, **env} if env else None)
    if check and r.returncode:
        sys.exit(f"session: `{' '.join(args)}` failed in {cwd}:\n{r.stderr.strip() or r.stdout.strip()}")
    return r


def git(cwd, *a, check=True, env=None):
    return run(["git", *a], cwd, check, env).stdout.strip()


# Every git read of a worktree that may be another session's runs with this (kit 20). A plain `git status`
# refreshes the index as a side effect: it takes the worktree's index.lock and holds it for its whole walk
# of the worktree, so the owning session's `git add` in that moment fails ("index.lock: File exists").
# With GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS=0 git skips that optional refresh and takes no lock; what it reports is the same.
# Cast, 2026-09-30: an archetype land's unchecked `git add` failed on its lock while other lanes ran
# `session status` and `session start`, and it committed half of its files (AR-04, AR-05).
READ_ONLY = {"GIT_OPTIONAL_LOCKS": "0"}


def commit(cwd, provider, *a):
    """Commit with AGENT_NAME set, for repos whose pre-commit checks handoff ownership.

    A land run by a session other than the one that started the worktree (a coordinator landing a lane that is
    gone) is a hand-off, not an interleave: the commit guard's shared-worktree check is passed for this one
    commit, the pin follows, and the land says so (kit 28)."""
    env = dict(os.environ, AGENT_NAME=os.environ.get("AGENT_NAME") or provider)
    pin, sid = read_pin(Path(cwd)), session_id()
    if pin and pin.get("session") and sid and pin["session"] != sid and env.get(ALLOW_SHARED) != "1":
        env[ALLOW_SHARED] = "1"
        print(f"note: this worktree was started by session {short_id(pin['session'])}; landing it as a hand-off")
    r = subprocess.run(["git", "commit", *a], cwd=cwd, text=True, capture_output=True, env=env)
    if r.returncode:
        sys.exit(f"session: commit failed in {cwd}:\n{(r.stderr or r.stdout).strip()}")


def repo_of(path):
    """(top, primary, common) of the git work tree that holds `path`, or None when it is in none.

    top is that work tree's root, primary the repository's own checkout (the parent of the common git
    dir, the same for every worktree of it), common the common git dir with symlinks resolved: two
    paths are one repository exactly when their common dirs are equal."""
    if path is None:
        return None
    r = run(["git", "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir"], path, check=False)
    lines = r.stdout.splitlines()
    if r.returncode or len(lines) < 2 or not lines[0].strip() or not lines[1].strip():
        return None
    common = Path(lines[1].strip())
    return Path(lines[0].strip()), common.parent, common.resolve()


# What this run reports to Witness (WS-DDR-150): the repo it acted on, counts and a reason, never content.
EVENT = {"verb": None, "target": None, "confidential": False, "attrs": {}}


def acted_on(primary):
    EVENT["target"] = primary.name
    EVENT["confidential"] = "Work" in primary.parts  # engagement repos: events carry the client-confidential tag


def here(allow_other=False):
    """(top, primary, linked): the checkout this command acts on, the repository's primary checkout, and
    whether top is a linked worktree.

    The repository is the one this script lives in (the parent of .agents/), never merely the one the shell
    happens to be standing in: on 2026-09-28 two lanes ran Cast's script from the Workspaces root, and each
    cut its worktree and branch in the governance repo without a word. Inside the script's own repository
    the shell's worktree is the one acted on, so the primary's copy of the script can drive a session
    worktree (Cortege does) and the reverse. A shell in a different repository is refused, in one line naming
    both, exit status 2, nothing touched; allow_other (--here) says the shell's repository is the one meant.
    A shell in no repository, or in a directory since removed, falls back to the script's own checkout."""
    own = repo_of(KIT_DIR)
    try:
        cwd = repo_of(Path.cwd())
    except OSError:  # the shell's directory is gone (finish removes the worktree it stood in)
        cwd = None
    if own and cwd and own[2] != cwd[2] and not allow_other:
        acted_on(own[1])
        EVENT["attrs"].update(reason="wrong_repo", shell_repo=cwd[1].name)
        print(f"session: this script belongs to the repository at {own[0]}, but the shell is in {cwd[0]}, a different "
              f"repository; run it from {own[0]} (a subshell leaves this shell where it is: (cd {own[0]} && python3 "
              f".agents/bin/session {EVENT.get('verb') or '<command>'} ...)), or pass --here to act on the repository the shell is in",
              file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(WRONG_REPO)
    found = cwd or own
    if not found:
        sys.exit("session: not inside a git repository")
    top, primary, _ = found
    acted_on(primary)
    return top, primary, top.resolve() != primary.resolve()


def witness_emit():
    """Witness's shared emit call, found the way org-design-tooling/src/witness_bridge.py finds it:
    $WITNESS_ROOT/src, the Core/ folder above this file, then ~/Workspaces/Core. None when Witness is not here."""
    roots = []
    if os.environ.get("WITNESS_ROOT"):
        roots.append(Path(os.environ["WITNESS_ROOT"]) / "src")
    for parent in Path(__file__).resolve().parents:
        if parent.name == "Core":
            roots.append(parent / "products" / "witness" / "src")
            break
    roots.append(Path.home() / "Workspaces" / "Core" / "products" / "witness" / "src")
    for src in roots:
        if (src / "emit.py").is_file():
            spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("witness_shared_emit", src / "emit.py")
            mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
            spec.loader.exec_module(mod)
            return mod.emit
    return None


def witness_report(cmd, code, started):
    """One event per run to Witness (WS-DDR-150): the verb, the repo, counts, a duration, never content.

    Fail-open by construction: Witness missing, a broken emit or a full disk leaves this command's output
    and exit status exactly as they were. Under pytest with no explicit WITNESS_INBOX nothing is written,
    so a test run never posts to the real inbox."""
    try:
        if os.environ.get("PYTEST_CURRENT_TEST") and not os.environ.get("WITNESS_INBOX"):
            return
        emit = witness_emit()
        if emit is None:
            return
        if EVENT.get("outcome"):
            outcome = EVENT["outcome"]  # a verb that always exits 0 but knows better (wip-backup's failed push)
        elif code in (0, None):
            outcome = "ok"
        elif code == 3 or EVENT["attrs"].get("reason") in ("wrong_repo", "low_disk", "batch_line", "kf11", "name_in_use",
                                                          *GUARD_REASONS):
            outcome = "blocked"  # stopped for a person: a rebase conflict, a shell in the wrong repo, a full disk, KF-11
        elif cmd == "status" and code == 1:
            outcome = "ok"  # overlaps found: a finding, not a failure
        else:
            outcome = "error"
        extra = {"redaction_level": "client-confidential"} if EVENT["confidential"] else {}
        with contextlib.redirect_stdout(io.StringIO()), contextlib.redirect_stderr(io.StringIO()):
            emit("org-design-tooling", "org_design_tooling.session_kit", outcome=outcome,
                 duration_ms=(time.monotonic() - started) * 1000.0, target=EVENT["target"],
                 verb=cmd, kit_version=KIT_VERSION, **EVENT["attrs"], **extra)
    except (Exception, SystemExit):  # an emit that calls sys.exit must not end this command either
        pass


def default_branch(repo):
    r = run(["git", "symbolic-ref", "--short", "refs/remotes/origin/HEAD"], repo, check=False)
    return r.stdout.strip().split("/", 1)[1] if r.returncode == 0 and "/" in r.stdout else "main"


def gh_repo(root):
    """owner/name of origin, so gh never guesses between origin and an upstream remote."""
    url = git(root, "remote", "get-url", "origin", check=False)
    m = re.search(r"github\.com[:/]([^/]+/[^/]+?)(?:\.git)?$", url)
    return ["-R", m.group(1)] if m else []


def policy(root):
    p = root / ".agents" / "repository-policy.json"
    try:
        return json.loads(p.read_text())
    except Exception:
        return {}


def task_files(root):
    d = root / ".agents" / "tasks"
    return sorted(d.glob("*.json")) if d.is_dir() else []


def my_task(root, base_ref):
    """This session's task file: the record whose "branch" names the branch checked out here, among the records this
    branch added, changed (a task name reused the same day: start rewrites the record base already holds) or has not
    committed yet. Kit 25: until then the first added record won, so a branch that also added an older record (a
    rename) closed that one and landed its own still live (governance root, 2026-10-02 and 2026-10-03). With no
    record naming the branch, the first added or untracked one, as before."""
    def names(*a):
        return [r for r in git(root, *a, "--", ".agents/tasks/", check=False, env=READ_ONLY).split() if r.endswith(".json")]
    added = names("diff", "--name-only", "--diff-filter=A", f"{base_ref}...HEAD")
    changed = names("diff", "--name-only", "--diff-filter=M", f"{base_ref}...HEAD") + names("diff", "--name-only", "HEAD")
    untracked = names("ls-files", "--others", "--exclude-standard")
    branch = git(root, "branch", "--show-current", check=False, env=READ_ONLY)
    if branch:
        for rel in dict.fromkeys(added + untracked + changed):
            try:
                if json.loads((root / rel).read_text()).get("branch") == branch:
                    return root / rel
            except (OSError, ValueError):
                continue
    for rel in added + untracked:
        return root / rel
    return None


def primary_behind(primary, base):
    """How many commits origin/<base> has that the primary's HEAD lacks (no fetch here)."""
    n = git(primary, "rev-list", "--count", f"HEAD..origin/{base}", check=False)
    return int(n) if n.isdigit() else 0


def ff_primary(primary, base):
    """Fast-forward the primary checkout to origin/<base>: the one place the kit ever moves it.

    Never force, never reset, never stash, never touch a primary that is not on its base branch:
    a parked branch, a detached HEAD or a colliding local edit all leave it alone and say so.
    Returns True when the primary now equals origin/<base>."""
    run(["git", "fetch", "-q", "origin", base], primary, check=False)
    on = git(primary, "branch", "--show-current", check=False)
    if on != base:
        print(f"primary NOT fast-forwarded: it is on {on or '(detached)'}, not {base}; it lags origin/{base} by "
              f"{primary_behind(primary, base)} until {base} is checked out and `session finish` runs")
        return False
    if primary_behind(primary, base) == 0:
        print(f"primary already at origin/{base} ({git(primary, 'rev-parse', '--short', 'HEAD', check=False)})")
        return True
    r = run(["git", "merge", "--ff-only", "-q", f"origin/{base}"], primary, check=False)
    if r.returncode:
        reason = " ".join((r.stderr or r.stdout).strip().split())[:200]
        print(f"primary NOT fast-forwarded: {reason}")
        return False
    print(f"primary fast-forwarded to {git(primary, 'rev-parse', '--short', 'HEAD', check=False)}")
    return True


# The kit's git hooks, one config hook each (kit 28 added the pre-commit guard beside the pre-push check).
# Each command tests for its hook file relative to the worktree it runs in, so a worktree cut from an older
# kit, which has no .agents/hooks/pre-commit, is left alone by a clone whose config already names the guard.
HOOKS = (("session-kit-portability", "pre-push", "the pre-push portability check"),
         ("session-kit-commit-guard", "pre-commit", "the commit guard"),
         ("session-kit-wip-backup", "post-commit", "the backup push after each commit"))


def hook_cmd(event):
    return f"test ! -f .agents/hooks/{event} || sh .agents/hooks/{event}"


def shim_mark(event):
    return f"# session-kit {event} shim"


def activate_hook(repo, name, event, what):
    """Turn on .agents/hooks/<event> for this clone without displacing any existing hook.

    Git 2.54+ config hooks run beside the hook directory's own hook (Entire, a repo's .githooks), so this
    only adds. On an older git, a .git/hooks/<event> shim is written only when core.hooksPath is unset and
    no such hook exists. Same logic as repo-kit/install.py."""
    cmd, mark = hook_cmd(event), shim_mark(event)
    have = git(repo, "config", "--get", f"hook.{name}.command", check=False)
    if have != cmd:
        git(repo, "config", f"hook.{name}.event", event, check=False)
        git(repo, "config", f"hook.{name}.command", cmd, check=False)
    listed = run(["git", "hook", "list", event], repo, check=False)
    if listed.returncode == 0 and name in listed.stdout:
        return
    still = "; `session land` still runs it" if event == "pre-push" else ""
    if git(repo, "config", "--get", "core.hooksPath", check=False):
        print(f"note: {what} not activated (core.hooksPath is set and this git has no config hooks){still}")
        return
    shim = Path(git(repo, "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir")) / "hooks" / event
    if shim.exists():
        if mark not in shim.read_text(errors="replace"):
            print(f"note: {what} not activated (a {event} hook exists and this git has no config hooks){still}")
        return
    shim.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    shim.write_text(f"#!/bin/sh\n{mark} (org-design-tooling/repo-kit)\n{cmd} \"$@\"\n")
    shim.chmod(0o755)


def activate_prepush(repo):
    """Turn on every kit hook for this clone: the pre-push check and, since kit 28, the pre-commit guard."""
    for name, event, what in HOOKS:
        activate_hook(repo, name, event, what)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The commit guard (kit 28): `session commit-guard`, run by .agents/hooks/pre-commit before every commit.
#
# Two failures it closes, both recorded as signals before it existed:
#   1. A commit on the primary checkout. On 2026-09-23 a session committed on the governance root's primary
#      main and swept a stale index, eight paths another session had staged and walked away from, into a
#      one-file change; main diverged, fixes already on origin were reverted locally, and every ff-only pull
#      refused until it was re-pointed. The Claude Code write guard blocks edits into a primary, but a commit
#      is a git command, which that guard never gates. Here: a commit in the primary checkout is refused,
#      the index is left exactly as it was, and the message names the staged count and `session start`.
#      A primary with no trunk yet (no origin/<base>, a repository being seeded) is not judged.
#   2. Two sessions in one working tree. On 2026-08-27 two sessions worked the same clone; a checkout in one
#      moved HEAD under the other, and from then on 25 commits from both landed on one branch in strict
#      alternation while every push reported success. `start` now pins the worktree to its branch and to
#      the session that started it (<git dir>/session-pin.json). A commit on another branch, or from another
#      session while the pin names one, is refused with the move and the remedy.
# Bypasses are per command, named in the refusal, and reported to Witness: SESSION_ALLOW_PRIMARY_COMMIT=1,
# SESSION_ALLOW_BRANCH_CHANGE=1 (re-pins to the branch you are on), SESSION_ALLOW_SHARED_WORKTREE=1 (re-pins
# to you; a hand-off from a session that is gone). Everything that goes wrong inside the guard fails open
# (main() turns an uncaught exception into exit 0 for this verb): a guard bug must never wedge every commit.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
PIN_FILE = "session-pin.json"
ALLOW_PRIMARY, ALLOW_BRANCH, ALLOW_SHARED = "SESSION_ALLOW_PRIMARY_COMMIT", "SESSION_ALLOW_BRANCH_CHANGE", "SESSION_ALLOW_SHARED_WORKTREE"
GUARD_REASONS = ("primary_commit", "branch_moved", "second_session")


def session_id():
    """The identity of the session running this command, when its harness says: Claude Code sets
    CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID; SESSION_KIT_SESSION_ID is for any other harness (and the tests). None when unknown."""
    return (os.environ.get("SESSION_KIT_SESSION_ID") or os.environ.get("CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID")
            or os.environ.get("CLAUDE_SESSION_ID") or None)


def pin_path(top):
    """This worktree's own git dir, which no checkout, rebase or clean reaches (as kept_dir)."""
    return Path(git(top, "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-dir", env=READ_ONLY)) / PIN_FILE


def read_pin(top):
    try:
        return json.loads(pin_path(top).read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError, SystemExit):
        return None


def write_pin(top, branch, task=None, sid=None):
    pin = {"branch": branch, "task": task, "session": sid,
           "pinned": dt.datetime.now().astimezone().isoformat(timespec="seconds")}
    p = pin_path(top)
    p.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    p.write_text(json.dumps(pin, indent=2) + "\n")
    return pin


def short_id(sid):
    return (sid or "unknown")[:8]


def cmd_commit_guard(a):
    """Exit 1 refuses the commit (git stops before it writes anything); 0 lets it go."""
    top, primary, linked = here(a.here)
    base = default_branch(primary)
    branch = git(top, "branch", "--show-current", check=False, env=READ_ONLY) or "(detached)"
    EVENT["attrs"].update(linked=linked, branch_kind="base" if branch == base else "other")
    if not linked:
        if not git(top, "rev-parse", "-q", "--verify", f"refs/remotes/origin/{base}", check=False, env=READ_ONLY):
            EVENT["attrs"]["decision"] = "no_trunk"  # a repository being seeded: nothing to follow yet
            return 0
        staged = name_list(top, "--cached", check=False, env=READ_ONLY)
        EVENT["attrs"]["staged"] = len(staged)
        if os.environ.get(ALLOW_PRIMARY) == "1":
            EVENT["attrs"]["bypass"] = "primary_commit"
            print(f"session: committing on the primary checkout of {primary.name} ({branch}) under {ALLOW_PRIMARY}=1; "
                  f"{len(staged)} staged path(s); reported to Witness", file=sys.stderr)
            return 0
        EVENT["attrs"]["reason"] = "primary_commit"
        print(f"session: REFUSED: this is the primary checkout of {primary.name}, on {branch}, and no commit is made "
              f"here (SESSIONS.md rule 1): every session shares it, and a commit here carries whatever anyone left "
              f"staged.\n  Staged here now: {len(staged)} path(s) (`git status --short` names them); nothing was "
              f"committed and the index is as it was.\n  Commit from a session worktree instead:\n"
              f"    python3 {primary}/.agents/bin/session start <task> --own <path>\n  then make the change there "
              f"(or `git stash push -u -m <tag>` here and apply it there), commit named files, and `session land`.\n"
              f"  A deliberate exception, reported to Witness: {ALLOW_PRIMARY}=1 git commit ...", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    pin = read_pin(top)
    if not pin:
        EVENT["attrs"]["decision"] = "unpinned"  # a worktree made before kit 28, or by hand
        return 0
    sid = session_id()
    if pin.get("branch") and pin["branch"] != branch:
        if os.environ.get(ALLOW_BRANCH) == "1":
            write_pin(top, branch, pin.get("task"), pin.get("session"))
            EVENT["attrs"]["bypass"] = "branch_change"
            print(f"session: this worktree was pinned to {pin['branch']} and is now on {branch}; re-pinned under "
                  f"{ALLOW_BRANCH}=1, reported to Witness", file=sys.stderr)
            return 0
        EVENT["attrs"]["reason"] = "branch_moved"
        print(f"session: REFUSED: this worktree was started on {pin['branch']} (task {pin.get('task') or 'unknown'}) "
              f"and HEAD is now on {branch}. A checkout moved under this session: on 2026-08-27 two sessions in one "
              f"working tree interleaved 25 commits onto one branch this way while every push said success.\n"
              f"  If you did not move it, another session is working in this worktree: start your own with\n"
              f"    python3 {primary}/.agents/bin/session start <task>\n  To go back: git switch {pin['branch']}\n"
              f"  If the move was yours and deliberate: {ALLOW_BRANCH}=1 git commit ... (re-pins to {branch}, "
              f"reported to Witness)", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    if pin.get("session") and sid and pin["session"] != sid:
        if os.environ.get(ALLOW_SHARED) == "1":
            write_pin(top, branch, pin.get("task"), sid)
            EVENT["attrs"]["bypass"] = "shared_worktree"
            print(f"session: this worktree was started by session {short_id(pin['session'])} and session "
                  f"{short_id(sid)} is committing in it; re-pinned under {ALLOW_SHARED}=1, reported to Witness",
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 0
        EVENT["attrs"]["reason"] = "second_session"
        print(f"session: REFUSED: this worktree belongs to session {short_id(pin['session'])} (task "
              f"{pin.get('task') or 'unknown'}, branch {branch}) and session {short_id(sid)} is committing in it. "
              f"Two live sessions in one working tree interleave their commits (2026-08-27, 25 commits on one branch).\n"
              f"  Start your own worktree:\n    python3 {primary}/.agents/bin/session start <task>\n"
              f"  If that session is gone and this is its hand-off: {ALLOW_SHARED}=1 git commit ... (re-pins this "
              f"worktree to you, reported to Witness)", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    return 0


PRE_LAND_TIMEOUT = 300       # seconds, for an entry that names none
PRE_LAND_TIMEOUT_MAX = 1800  # a land is not a place for a long suite
PRE_LAND_TAIL = 40           # lines of a command's output the land shows


def pre_land_commands(top):
    """The repository's own pre-land commands (kit 18): the list under "pre_land" in the worktree's
    .agents/repository-policy.json, each {"name": str, "run": [argument, ...], "timeout_s": seconds}.
    `{python}` in run is the interpreter running this script; a relative path is relative to the worktree.
    Returns [(name, argv, timeout)], [] when none are declared.

    Fails closed, exit status 1: a policy file that does not parse, or an entry without a name, a non-empty
    list of string arguments and a timeout between 1 and PRE_LAND_TIMEOUT_MAX seconds, stops the land. It is
    read here and not through policy(), which reads an unreadable file as no policy: for a gate, that would
    turn the gate off without a word (Cast pipeline gap closure, AUD-30, 2026-09-30)."""
    p = top / ".agents" / "repository-policy.json"
    if not p.is_file():
        return []
    try:
        pol = json.loads(p.read_text())
    except ValueError as e:
        EVENT["attrs"]["reason"] = "pre_land"
        sys.exit(f"session: .agents/repository-policy.json does not parse ({e}), so its pre_land gate cannot be "
                 "read; fix the file and land again")
    raw = pol.get("pre_land", []) if isinstance(pol, dict) else None
    if not isinstance(raw, list):
        EVENT["attrs"]["reason"] = "pre_land"
        sys.exit("session: pre_land in .agents/repository-policy.json must be a list of commands; fix it and land again")
    out = []
    for i, e in enumerate(raw):
        e = e if isinstance(e, dict) else {}
        name, argv, limit = e.get("name"), e.get("run"), e.get("timeout_s", PRE_LAND_TIMEOUT)
        ok = (isinstance(name, str) and name.strip() and isinstance(argv, list) and argv
              and all(isinstance(x, str) and x for x in argv)
              and isinstance(limit, (int, float)) and not isinstance(limit, bool) and 1 <= limit <= PRE_LAND_TIMEOUT_MAX)
        if not ok:
            label = name.strip() if isinstance(name, str) and name.strip() else f"entry {i + 1}"
            EVENT["attrs"].update(reason="pre_land", pre_land=label[:80])
            sys.exit(f"session: pre_land {label!r} in .agents/repository-policy.json needs a name, a non-empty \"run\" "
                     f"list of arguments and a timeout_s from 1 to {PRE_LAND_TIMEOUT_MAX} seconds; fix the declaration "
                     "and land again")
        out.append((name.strip(), [sys.executable if x == "{python}" else x for x in argv], float(limit)))
    return out


def run_pre_land(top, commands):
    """Run each pre-land command in the worktree, in order, and stop the land (exit status 1, never 3, which
    callers read as a rebase conflict) at the first that exits non-zero or outlives its timeout. The last
    PRE_LAND_TAIL lines of its output are shown whichever way it ends. A command that times out is killed
    with its whole process group, so a test runner's children do not outlive the land."""
    for name, argv, limit in commands:
        started = time.monotonic()
        try:
            proc = subprocess.Popen(argv, cwd=top, text=True, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT,
                                    env=dict(os.environ, SESSION_PRE_LAND=name), start_new_session=True)
        except OSError as e:
            EVENT["attrs"].update(reason="pre_land", pre_land=name[:80])
            sys.exit(f"session: pre-land check {name!r} failed (it could not start: {e}); fix it and land again")
        try:
            output, _ = proc.communicate(timeout=limit)
            why = f"exit {proc.returncode}"
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            try:
                os.killpg(proc.pid, signal.SIGKILL)
            except OSError:
                proc.kill()
            output, _ = proc.communicate()
            why = f"timed out after {limit:g} s"
        lines = (output or "").strip().splitlines()
        if lines:
            print(f"pre-land check {name!r}:" + ("" if len(lines) <= PRE_LAND_TAIL else f" (last {PRE_LAND_TAIL} lines)"))
            print("\n".join("  " + x for x in lines[-PRE_LAND_TAIL:]))
        if why == "exit 0":
            print(f"pre-land check {name!r} passed ({time.monotonic() - started:.1f} s)")
            EVENT["attrs"]["pre_land_passed"] = EVENT["attrs"].get("pre_land_passed", 0) + 1
            continue
        EVENT["attrs"].update(reason="pre_land", pre_land=name[:80])
        sys.exit(f"session: pre-land check {name!r} failed ({why}); fix it and land again (the repository's own "
                 "gate, declared under pre_land in .agents/repository-policy.json)")


def portability_check(top):
    """The pre-push check, run by land before it pushes. Returns True when it passes or is absent."""
    chk = top / ".agents" / "bin" / "portability-check"
    if not chk.is_file():
        return True
    r = run([sys.executable, str(chk)], top, check=False)
    out = (r.stdout + r.stderr).strip()
    if out:
        print(out)
    if r.returncode == 0:
        # the pre-push hook sees this and skips re-running the check on the same commit
        os.environ["SESSION_PORTABILITY_CHECKED"] = git(top, "rev-parse", "HEAD")
    return r.returncode == 0


def follow(top, landed, head, base):
    """Point the session branch at the commit that landed it. Only when the trees match, so the
    working tree and index stay as they are; compare-and-swap on the old head, never a reset."""
    if git(top, "rev-parse", "-q", "--verify", f"{landed}^{{tree}}", check=False) != git(top, "rev-parse", f"{head}^{{tree}}"):
        return False
    r = run(["git", "update-ref", "-m", f"session land: follow the landing on origin/{base}", "HEAD", landed, head], top, check=False)
    if r.returncode == 0:
        print(f"branch now at {landed[:8]}: a later land from this worktree replays only new commits")
    return r.returncode == 0


def has_gh():
    return shutil.which("gh") is not None


def landed_boundary(top, base, branch, up=None):
    """Where an earlier landing from this branch ends: (newest landed commit of ours, rebase target,
    why) or None. Kit 11 and earlier left the branch on its unsquashed commits after a landing, and
    an auto-merged PR still does; rebasing those onto origin/<base> replays them onto their own
    squash, and batches that touched the same lines conflict with themselves.

    A local landing's squash has the tree of the branch head it landed, so a commit on origin/<base>
    with the same tree as one of ours marks it. A PR that GitHub squashed after origin/<base> moved
    has a tree of its own; for a branch that was pushed for a PR, GitHub says which head it merged."""
    up = up or f"origin/{base}"
    mine = [l.split() for l in git(top, "log", "--format=%H %T", f"{up}..HEAD", check=False).splitlines()]
    if not mine:
        return None
    landed = {}
    for h, t in (l.split() for l in git(top, "log", "--reverse", "--format=%H %T", f"HEAD..{up}", check=False).splitlines()):
        landed.setdefault(t, h)
    for h, t in mine:  # newest first
        if t in landed:
            return h, landed[t], f"its tree is {landed[t][:8]} on origin/{base}"
    if branch and has_gh() and git(top, "config", "--get", f"branch.{branch}.merge", check=False):
        r = run(["gh", "pr", "list", *gh_repo(top), "--head", branch, "--state", "merged",
                 "--json", "number,headRefOid", "--limit", "50"], top, check=False)
        try:
            heads = {x["headRefOid"]: x["number"] for x in json.loads(r.stdout or "[]")} if r.returncode == 0 else {}
        except (ValueError, KeyError, TypeError):
            heads = {}
        for h, _ in mine:
            if h in heads:
                return h, up, f"PR #{heads[h]} merged it"
    return None


def kept_dir(top):
    """Where land keeps a copy of each file the branch untracks while a rebase runs: this worktree's
    own git dir, which no checkout, rebase or clean reaches. `git worktree remove` deletes it with
    the worktree, so finish refuses while anything is left in it."""
    return Path(git(top, "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-dir")) / "session-kept"


def names(paths, n=8):
    return ", ".join(paths[:n]) + (f" and {len(paths) - n} more" if len(paths) > n else "")


def tree_paths(top, ref):
    return set(run(["git", "ls-tree", "-r", "-z", "--name-only", ref], top, check=False).stdout.split("\0")) - {""}


def keep_untracked(top, base, onto=None, up=None):
    """Copy aside each file this branch untracks (`git rm --cached`) that is still on disk and that
    the rebase target still tracks. The rebase checks the target out first, git overwrites an
    ignored file there without asking, and replaying the untrack commit then deletes it: the loss
    `git rm --cached` promises never happens (an engagement repo, 2026-09-24). Returns the paths kept."""
    up = up or f"origin/{base}"
    mb = git(top, "merge-base", "HEAD", up, check=False)
    if not mb:
        return []
    gone = run(["git", "diff", "--name-only", "-z", "--no-renames", "--diff-filter=D", mb, "HEAD"], top, check=False).stdout
    on_disk = [p for p in gone.split("\0") if p and ((top / p).is_symlink() or (top / p).is_file())]
    if not on_disk:
        return []
    tracked = set().union(*(tree_paths(top, ref) for ref in {up, onto or up}))
    kept = [p for p in on_disk if p in tracked]
    kd = kept_dir(top)
    clash = [p for p in kept if os.path.lexists(kd / p)]
    if clash:
        sys.exit(f"session: an earlier land still keeps a copy of {names(clash)} in {kd}, and this rebase would replace "
                 "the file again. Merge the kept copy with the one in the worktree by hand, delete the kept copy, then land again")
    for p in kept:
        (kd / p).parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        shutil.copy2(top / p, kd / p, follow_symlinks=False)
    return kept


def same(a, b):
    if a.is_symlink() or b.is_symlink():
        return a.is_symlink() and b.is_symlink() and os.readlink(a) == os.readlink(b)
    return b.is_file() and filecmp.cmp(a, b, shallow=False)


def restore_untracked(top):
    """Put back what keep_untracked copied aside, only where nothing is lost: the path is untracked at
    HEAD and holds nothing, or holds the same thing. Returns (restored, [(path, why) still kept]),
    why being "tracked" (HEAD tracks the path) or "differs" (something wrote it after the rebase)."""
    kd = kept_dir(top)
    if not kd.is_dir():
        return [], []
    saved = []
    for d, dirs, files in os.walk(kd):
        saved += [Path(d) / x for x in files + [x for x in dirs if (Path(d) / x).is_symlink()]]
    head = tree_paths(top, "HEAD") if saved else set()
    restored, left = [], []
    for src in sorted(saved):
        rel = src.relative_to(kd).as_posix()
        dst = top / rel
        if rel in head:
            left.append((rel, "tracked"))
        elif not os.path.lexists(dst):
            dst.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
            try:
                os.replace(src, dst)
            except OSError:  # another filesystem
                shutil.copy2(src, dst, follow_symlinks=False)
                os.unlink(src)
            restored.append(rel)
        elif same(src, dst):
            os.unlink(src)
            restored.append(rel)
        else:
            left.append((rel, "differs"))
    for d, _, _ in sorted(os.walk(kd), reverse=True):  # deepest first: drop the folders emptied
        if not os.listdir(d):
            os.rmdir(d)
    return restored, left


def put_back(top, kept=(), stopped=False):
    """Restore the kept copies and say what happened. Returns the paths still kept."""
    restored, left = restore_untracked(top)
    kd = kept_dir(top) if left else None
    now = [p for p in kept if p in restored]
    if now:
        print(f"kept {len(now)} file(s) this branch untracks through the rebase: {names(now)}")
    earlier = [p for p in restored if p not in kept]
    if earlier:
        print(f"restored {names(earlier)}: an earlier land kept it aside during its rebase")
    for rel, why in left:
        if why == "tracked" and stopped:
            print(f"kept aside until the rebase finishes: {rel} (copy at {kd / rel}); the next `session land` puts it back")
        elif why == "tracked":
            print(f"NOT restored: {rel} is tracked at HEAD again; the copy land kept is at {kd / rel}")
        else:
            print(f"NOT restored: {rel} was written again after the rebase, and neither copy is the kit's to discard; "
                  f"the copy land kept is at {kd / rel}")
    if left and not stopped:
        print(f"Merge each by hand, then delete the kept copy; `session finish` waits until {kd} is empty.")
    return [rel for rel, _ in left]


CONFLICT = {}  # what the last stopped rebase met: files, the other people's landings, and whether the land was refused


def conflict_landings(top, mb, pin, files):
    """The landings on the base since this branch left it (mb..pin) that changed the conflicted files, newest first:
    each one's commit, author, date, subject and, for a local landing, the session branch it came from."""
    if not (mb and files):
        return []
    out = git(top, "log", "--format=%H%x1f%an%x1f%ae%x1f%cI%x1f%s%x1f%b%x1e", f"{mb}..{pin}", "--", *files, check=False,
              env=READ_ONLY)
    rows = []
    for rec in out.split("\x1e"):
        parts = rec.strip("\n").split("\x1f")
        if len(parts) < 6 or not parts[0].strip():
            continue
        m = LANDED_FROM.search(parts[5])
        rows.append({"commit": parts[0].strip(), "author": parts[1], "email": parts[2], "date": parts[3],
                     "subject": parts[4], "branch": m.group(1) if m else ""})
    return rows


def my_identity(top):
    """(name, email) this clone commits as, from `git var GIT_AUTHOR_IDENT`; ("", "") when it cannot be read."""
    m = re.match(r"(.*) <([^>]*)>", git(top, "var", "GIT_AUTHOR_IDENT", check=False))
    return (m.group(1).strip(), m.group(2).strip().lower()) if m else ("", "")


def someone_elses(rows, me):
    """The landings whose author is not this clone's identity. A landing counts as this person's own when its author
    email OR its author name equals this clone's: a pull request GitHub squash-merged carries the account's name with
    the same email, or the same name with a noreply email, and one person's own sessions must keep the solo path."""
    name, mail = me
    if not (name or mail):
        return []
    return [r for r in rows if not ((mail and r["email"].lower() == mail) or (name and r["author"] == name))]


def rebase_on_base(top, base, branch, decided=""):
    """Rebase the session branch onto origin/<base>, first skipping commits that already landed.
    Returns the commit it rebased onto (a sha, truthy), or reports the conflict and returns "" (the
    rebase stays open to resolve). Files the branch untracks are kept through it (keep_untracked); a
    rebase with nothing to do touches no file, so they are copied only when origin/<base> moved or
    commits are skipped.

    Kit 19: origin/<base> is read ONCE, here, and the rebase goes onto that commit. Every worktree of a
    repository shares refs/remotes/origin/<base>, and a sibling session's `git push` moves it; the land
    must build its squash on the commit this branch was rebased onto, never on whatever the shared ref
    names later (Cast e2bcc5811 and 466d01305, 2026-09-30, reverted two sessions' landings that way)."""
    skipped = ""
    CONFLICT.clear()
    pin = git(top, "rev-parse", "--verify", f"origin/{base}^{{commit}}")
    left_at = git(top, "merge-base", "HEAD", pin, check=False)
    b = landed_boundary(top, base, branch, up=pin)
    moving = b or run(["git", "merge-base", "--is-ancestor", pin, "HEAD"], top, check=False).returncode
    kept = keep_untracked(top, base, b[1] if b else None, up=pin) if moving else []
    if b:
        c, onto, why = b
        n = git(top, "rev-list", "--count", f"{pin}..{c}")
        rest = git(top, "rev-list", "--count", f"{c}..HEAD")
        skipped = f"{n} commit(s) on this branch already landed ({why})"
        print(f"{skipped}; replaying only the {rest} after them")
        r = run(["git", "rebase", "--onto", onto, c], top, check=False)
    if not b or r.returncode == 0:
        mb = git(top, "merge-base", "HEAD", pin, check=False)
        r = run(["git", "rebase", pin], top, check=False)
        if r.returncode == 0:
            put_back(top, kept)
            return pin
    else:
        mb = ""
    files = git(top, "diff", "--name-only", "--diff-filter=U", check=False)
    # Kit 40 (M27): a conflict with ANOTHER PERSON's landing is two owners' intents that cannot both hold, and no
    # agent chooses between them. Until kit 39 the hint here told the stopped session to "resolve by meaning", the
    # other person was not named, and the stopped task's record already read complete (the 2026-11-17 trial, P7).
    conflicted = name_list(top, "--diff-filter=U", check=False)
    me = my_identity(top)
    theirs = someone_elses(conflict_landings(top, left_at, pin, conflicted), me)
    if theirs:
        named = "\n".join(f"  {r['author']} <{r['email']}> landed {r['commit'][:10]} on {r['date']}: {r['subject']!r}"
                           + (f" (from {r['branch']})" if r["branch"] else "") for r in theirs[:5])
        CONFLICT.update(files=conflicted, landings=theirs, refused=not decided,
                        me=f"{me[0]} <{me[1]}>".strip())
        owners = " and ".join(dict.fromkeys([me[0] or "this task's owner"] + [r["author"] for r in theirs]))
        if decided:
            print(f"REBASE CONFLICT with someone else's landing, on the same lines:\n{files}\n{named}\n\n"
                  f"The owners' decision on record for this land: {decided!r}.\nApply that decision to these lines and "
                  "nothing else. Then `git add <files>`, `git rebase --continue`, and run\n"
                  "`python3 .agents/bin/session land` again with the same --owners-decided.")
            put_back(top, kept, stopped=True)
            return ""
        run(["git", "rebase", "--abort"], top, check=False)
        print(f"LAND REFUSED. This work and someone else's landing change the same lines:\n{files}\n{named}\n\n"
              f"These are two owners' intents, and no agent chooses between them. The two owners decide: {owners} "
              "(or whoever is authorized to decide for them).\nNothing was chosen and nothing was lost: the rebase was "
              "undone, this branch and its commits are as they were, and the task stays unlanded.\nWhen the owners "
              "have decided, land again with their decision on the record:\n"
              "  python3 .agents/bin/session land --owners-decided \"<who decided, and what>\"\n"
              "That land stops at the same lines with the rebase left open, to apply the decision and nothing else.")
        put_back(top, kept)
        return ""
    print("REBASE CONFLICT. Another session landed changes to the same lines:\n" + files)
    if skipped:
        print(f"(Not a replay of this branch's own landing: {skipped} and were skipped first.)")
    elif mb and branch:
        mine = git(top, "log", "--format=%h", "-F", f"--grep={branch}", f"{mb}..origin/{base}", check=False).split()
        if mine:
            print(f"Check first: {', '.join(mine)} on origin/{base} names this branch. If that is this branch's own "
                  f"earlier landing, `git rebase --abort`, then `git rebase --onto origin/{base} <the last commit "
                  "of yours it carries>`, and land again.")
    print("\nResolve by meaning: keep both sessions' intents, never drop theirs to make yours apply.\n"
          "Then `git add <files>`, `git rebase --continue`, and run `python3 .agents/bin/session land` again.")
    put_back(top, kept, stopped=True)
    return ""


def pushed(top, rev="HEAD"):
    """True when rev is already on some remote-tracking branch (never amend a pushed commit)."""
    return bool(git(top, "branch", "-r", "--contains", rev, check=False))


# Free-disk guard on `start` (kit 21). On 2026-10-02 Aurelius fell to 2 GB free: about 105 session worktrees
# had piled up across the repos (0.65 to 3.5 GB each, 3.4 GB for the largest engagement repo) and nothing said a
# word when the next one was cut. `start` now measures the disk that will hold the new worktree, estimates the
# checkout from the base tree's blob sizes (never a walk of the primary), refuses under a floor and warns under
# a softer line. Fail-open: a measurement that errors prints one note and the start goes on.
GB = 1024 ** 3
MIN_FREE_GB, WARN_FREE_GB = 10.0, 25.0  # SESSION_MIN_FREE_GB, SESSION_WARN_FREE_GB override; SESSION_ALLOW_LOW_DISK=1 overrides once


def _env_gb(name, default):
    v = os.environ.get(name, "").strip()
    return float(v) if v else default


def checkout_estimate(repo, ref, block=1):
    """Bytes a checkout of `ref` writes: the sum of its blob sizes from `git ls-tree -r -l -z`, read as bytes so
    no file name can fail to decode. A gitlink (a submodule, size "-") is skipped; so is any other entry git
    gives no size. An LFS file counts as its pointer, so this can read low for an LFS repository.
    block (kit 41): round each blob up to that allocation unit, which is what the disk really gives a file."""
    r = subprocess.run(["git", "ls-tree", "-r", "-l", "-z", ref], cwd=repo, capture_output=True,
                       env={**os.environ, **READ_ONLY})
    if r.returncode:
        raise RuntimeError(f"git ls-tree exited {r.returncode}")
    total = 0
    for entry in r.stdout.split(b"\0"):
        meta = entry.partition(b"\t")[0].split()
        if len(meta) == 4 and meta[1] == b"blob" and meta[3] != b"-":
            total += -(-int(meta[3]) // block) * block
    return total


def linked_worktrees(primary):
    """How many linked worktrees the repository has (every entry of `git worktree list` but the primary)."""
    out = git(primary, "worktree", "list", "--porcelain", env=READ_ONLY)
    return max(0, sum(1 for line in out.splitlines() if line.startswith("worktree ")) - 1)


def disk_guard(primary, where, ref, adopt):
    """Refuse (exit, nothing created), warn, or pass before `start` cuts a worktree at `where` from `ref`.

    adopt (`--here`): nothing new is checked out, so it only ever warns. The Witness event gets the numbers
    (free_gb, estimate_gb) and the result (disk_guard: guard_ok, guard_warn, guard_refused, guard_overridden,
    or guard_skipped when the measurement failed), never a path. The key is disk_guard, not outcome: the
    event's own outcome field is witness_report's, and a second one would make the emit call raise."""
    try:
        floor = _env_gb("SESSION_MIN_FREE_GB", MIN_FREE_GB)
        soft = _env_gb("SESSION_WARN_FREE_GB", WARN_FREE_GB)
        free = shutil.disk_usage(where).free
        estimate = 0 if adopt else checkout_estimate(primary, ref)
    except Exception as e:  # fail-open: the guard never stops a start it could not measure
        EVENT["attrs"]["disk_guard"] = "guard_skipped"
        print(f"session: note: free-disk check skipped ({type(e).__name__}: {e}); starting without it", file=sys.stderr)
        return
    after = (free - estimate) / GB
    EVENT["attrs"].update(free_gb=round(free / GB, 2), estimate_gb=round(estimate / GB, 2))
    if after >= soft and (adopt or after >= floor):
        EVENT["attrs"]["disk_guard"] = "guard_ok"
        return
    try:
        n = linked_worktrees(primary)
    except BaseException:  # the count only decorates the message
        n = "an unknown number of"
    numbers = (f"{free / GB:.1f} GB free on this disk (adopting this worktree checks out nothing new)" if adopt else
               f"{free / GB:.1f} GB free on the disk that would hold it, the checkout needs about "
               f"{estimate / GB:.2f} GB, leaving {after:.1f} GB")
    remedy = (f"This repository has {n} linked worktree(s). See them with `python3 .agents/bin/session status`, "
              f"and run `python3 .agents/bin/session finish` in each one that has landed. The hourly worktree "
              f"reaper (com.brien.worktree-reaper) retires landed, idle worktrees on its own.")
    if not adopt and after < floor:
        if os.environ.get("SESSION_ALLOW_LOW_DISK", "").strip() == "1":
            EVENT["attrs"]["disk_guard"] = "guard_overridden"
            print(f"session: WARNING: LOW DISK, starting anyway because SESSION_ALLOW_LOW_DISK=1: {numbers}, "
                  f"under the {floor:g} GB floor (SESSION_MIN_FREE_GB). {remedy}", file=sys.stderr)
            return
        EVENT["attrs"].update(disk_guard="guard_refused", reason="low_disk")
        sys.exit(f"session: refusing to cut a new worktree, nothing was created: {numbers}, under the {floor:g} GB "
                 f"floor (SESSION_MIN_FREE_GB).\n{remedy}\nTo start anyway, once: "
                 f"SESSION_ALLOW_LOW_DISK=1 python3 .agents/bin/session start <task> ...")
    EVENT["attrs"]["disk_guard"] = "guard_warn"
    print(f"session: WARNING: LOW DISK: {numbers}, under the {soft:g} GB warning line (SESSION_WARN_FREE_GB). "
          f"{remedy}", file=sys.stderr)


# Batch lanes (kit 41). On 2026-11-18 a persona batch cut fourteen worktrees of one repository up front, and its
# fetch step cut one more for every landing. Each start passed the floor on its own, but the repository's checkout
# grew from 1.4 to 5 GB within a day as the batch's own pages landed, and the disk sat at 7 to 9 GB free for hours
# (6.9 GB on 2026-11-19, ten such worktrees holding 44 GB). The floor judges one start; nothing judged the batch.
# A batch lane is parallel width, not need, so it may only spend space above the batch line. The room between that
# line and the floor stays for the worktrees that move work to landing (a fetch step's land worktree, a close) and
# for swap, which lives on the same volume. A lane that does not fit is queued, not failed: exit QUEUED, nothing made.
BLOCK = 4096  # the allocation unit: a 90,000-file checkout measured 5.0 GB on disk against 4.85 GB of blobs
QUEUED = 75   # EX_TEMPFAIL: the lane waits in its batch's queue and is started once a running lane has finished


def batch_line():
    """GB a batch lane must leave free: SESSION_BATCH_FREE_GB, else the warning line (25 unless moved)."""
    return _env_gb("SESSION_BATCH_FREE_GB", _env_gb("SESSION_WARN_FREE_GB", WARN_FREE_GB))


def lane_room(primary, ref):
    """(free bytes on the disk that holds this repository's sibling worktrees, bytes one more lane of `ref` takes)."""
    return shutil.disk_usage(primary.parent).free, checkout_estimate(primary, ref, block=BLOCK)


def batch_guard(primary, ref, batch):
    """Queue (exit QUEUED, nothing created) or admit a batch lane before `start` cuts it from `ref`. Fail-open like
    the floor: a measurement that errors prints one note, and the floor still judges the start. The Witness event
    gets lane_gb, batch_line_gb and batch_guard (batch_admitted, batch_queued or batch_skipped), never the batch
    name or a path."""
    EVENT["attrs"]["batch"] = True
    try:
        line = batch_line()
        free, lane = lane_room(primary, ref)
    except Exception as e:
        EVENT["attrs"]["batch_guard"] = "batch_skipped"
        print(f"session: note: batch-line check skipped ({type(e).__name__}: {e}); the floor still applies",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return
    after = (free - lane) / GB
    EVENT["attrs"].update(lane_gb=round(lane / GB, 2), batch_line_gb=line)
    if after >= line:
        EVENT["attrs"]["batch_guard"] = "batch_admitted"
        return
    EVENT["attrs"].update(batch_guard="batch_queued", reason="batch_line")
    print(f"session: batch {batch}: lane not cut, queue it (nothing was created): {free / GB:.1f} GB free, a lane of "
          f"this repository takes about {lane / GB:.2f} GB, leaving {after:.1f} GB, under the {line:g} GB batch line "
          f"(SESSION_BATCH_FREE_GB). A batch lane spends only the space above that line; the room under it is kept "
          f"for landing work and for swap, and SESSION_ALLOW_LOW_DISK does not admit a batch lane. Start this lane "
          f"once a running lane has landed and `python3 .agents/bin/session finish` has freed its checkout; "
          f"`python3 .agents/bin/session capacity --lanes N` says how many fit now.", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(QUEUED)


def cmd_capacity(a):
    """How many more lanes of this repository fit above the batch line now (kit 41), so a batch cuts that many and
    queues the rest. Read-only but for fetching the base branch, so the estimate is the tree a new lane would check
    out. Exit 0 when all `--lanes` fit, QUEUED when some must wait, 1 when it could not measure: a planner is told
    so, never handed a guess, and each `start --batch` is still judged as it is cut."""
    _, primary, _ = here(a.here)
    if a.lanes < 1:
        sys.exit("session: capacity: --lanes must be 1 or more")
    base = default_branch(primary)
    git(primary, "fetch", "-q", "origin", base, check=False)  # offline: estimate the base branch this clone has
    try:
        line, floor = batch_line(), _env_gb("SESSION_MIN_FREE_GB", MIN_FREE_GB)
        free, lane = lane_room(primary, f"origin/{base}")
    except Exception as e:
        print(f"session: capacity unmeasured ({type(e).__name__}: {e}); start lanes one at a time with --batch, each "
              f"is judged as it is cut", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    fits = a.lanes if lane <= 0 else max(0, int((free - line * GB) // lane))
    start = min(fits, a.lanes)
    queue = a.lanes - start
    EVENT["attrs"].update(free_gb=round(free / GB, 2), lane_gb=round(lane / GB, 2), batch_line_gb=line,
                          lanes=a.lanes, fits=fits, queue=queue)
    if a.json:
        print(json.dumps({"free_gb": round(free / GB, 2), "lane_gb": round(lane / GB, 2), "batch_line_gb": line,
                          "floor_gb": floor, "fits": fits, "lanes": a.lanes, "start": start, "queue": queue}))
    else:
        print(f"capacity: {free / GB:.1f} GB free; a lane of this repository takes about {lane / GB:.2f} GB; the batch "
              f"line is {line:g} GB (SESSION_BATCH_FREE_GB). Room for {fits} lane(s) now: start {start} of "
              f"{a.lanes}, queue {queue}.")
        if queue:
            print("Start a queued lane once a running one has landed and `python3 .agents/bin/session finish` has "
                  "freed its checkout, then ask again.")
    EVENT["outcome"] = "ok"  # a count, not a failure, whichever way it comes out
    return QUEUED if queue else 0


ALLOW_NAME = "SESSION_ALLOW_NAME_IN_USE"


def name_in_use_check(primary, base, branch, tid, task):
    """Kit 39: refuse a task name someone else already uses, before anything is created. On origin, a branch or a
    backup ref of this branch's name, or this task's record on origin/<base>, that this clone did not make (its
    own-work log, its local branch and reflog) means another clone holds the name: two clones on one name share one
    branch, one task record and one backup ref, and their lands collide (the 2026-11-17 two-person trial, P1). The
    refusal names the holder and its age. The same clone restarting its own task passes. Fail-open: an origin that
    cannot be asked prints one note and the start goes on. SESSION_ALLOW_NAME_IN_USE=1 starts anyway, once."""
    if os.environ.get(ALLOW_NAME, "").strip() == "1":
        EVENT["attrs"]["bypass"] = "name_in_use"
        return
    try:
        rt = load_retirement()
        if rt.own_started(primary, branch):
            return
        r = run(["git", "ls-remote", "origin", f"refs/heads/{branch}", rt.wip_ref(branch)], primary, check=False,
                env=wip_env())
        if r.returncode:
            print("session: note: origin could not be asked whether this task name is in use; starting without that "
                  "check", file=sys.stderr)
            return
        found = []
        for line in r.stdout.splitlines():
            sha, _, ref = line.partition("\t")
            if not sha or rt.sha_owned(primary, ref, sha, branch):
                continue
            if not rt.has_commit(primary, sha):
                rt.fetch_tip(primary, ref, env=wip_env())
            who, when = rt.tip_owner(primary, sha)
            found.append(f"{ref} (tip {sha[:10]} by {who}, {rt.age_text(when)})")
        rel = f".agents/tasks/{tid}.json"
        blob = run(["git", "show", f"origin/{base}:{rel}"], primary, check=False, env=READ_ONLY)
        if blob.returncode == 0:
            try:
                rec = json.loads(blob.stdout)
            except ValueError:
                rec = {}
            log = git(primary, "log", "-1", "--format=%an%x1f%cI", f"origin/{base}", "--", rel, check=False,
                      env=READ_ONLY).split("\x1f")
            who = log[0] if log and log[0] else "an unknown author"
            when = rec.get("started") if isinstance(rec, dict) and rec.get("started") else (log[1] if len(log) > 1 else "")
            found.append(f"the task record {rel} on origin/{base} (committed by {who}, task {rt.age_text(when)})")
    except (Exception, SystemExit) as e:  # noqa: BLE001 - the check never stops a start it could not run
        print(f"session: note: the task-name check could not run ({type(e).__name__}); starting without it", file=sys.stderr)
        return
    if found:
        EVENT["attrs"]["reason"] = "name_in_use"
        sys.exit(f"session: refused: the task name {task!r} is already in use on origin, and this clone did not start "
                 f"it: {'; '.join(found)}. Nothing was created.\n  Two clones on one name share one branch, one task "
                 f"record and one backup ref, and their lands collide. Pick another task name"
                 f"{NAME_HINT}.\n  If the name is yours from another clone and you mean to reuse it, once: "
                 f"{ALLOW_NAME}=1 python3 .agents/bin/session start {task} ...")


def claim_at_start(primary, branch, tid, task, person):
    """Kit 40: claim the new branch's name on the remote before anything is created, so two clones that start one name
    before either has saved cannot both go on (retirement.claim_name: a create-only push of a small claim commit to
    refs/claims/<branch>; the remote takes exactly one). The loser is refused and told the holder and the claim's age.
    Returns the remote the claim was made on, or "" when this call made none. A claim this clone already holds (an
    earlier start of the name, or one that died after its claim) passes. Fail-open like the kit 39 check: a remote
    that cannot be asked, or an origin that takes no unlanded work (kit 37) with no private backup remote, prints at
    most one note and the start goes on. SESSION_ALLOW_NAME_IN_USE=1 makes no claim and steps past another's, once."""
    if os.environ.get(ALLOW_NAME, "").strip() == "1":
        return ""
    try:
        rt = load_retirement()
        remote, vis = rt.backup_destination(primary)
        if remote is None:
            # kit 37's gate: nothing unlanded, a task's name included, goes to an origin that is public or unknown
            EVENT["attrs"].update(claim="kept_local", origin_visibility=vis)
            if vis != "public":
                print(f"session: note: the task name was not claimed: origin's visibility is {vis} just now and no "
                      "private backup remote is named; starting without a claim", file=sys.stderr)
            return ""
        result, detail = rt.claim_name(primary, branch, tid, person, remote=remote, env=wip_env())
    except (Exception, SystemExit) as e:  # noqa: BLE001 - the claim never stops a start it could not run
        print(f"session: note: the task name could not be claimed ({type(e).__name__}); starting without a claim", file=sys.stderr)
        return ""
    EVENT["attrs"]["claim"] = result
    if result == "held":
        EVENT["attrs"]["reason"] = "name_in_use"
        sys.exit(f"session: refused: the task name {task!r} is already in use, and this clone did not claim it: {detail}. "
                 f"Nothing was created.\n  Another clone started this name first; the remote takes one claim per name. "
                 f"Pick another task name{NAME_HINT}; `python3 .agents/bin/session status --all` lists what other clones hold, and `session resume` continues another clone's saved work as a new attempt.\n  A claim whose start died is taken up again when its own clone "
                 f"starts the name again. To step past a claim that will never be used, once: "
                 f"{ALLOW_NAME}=1 python3 .agents/bin/session start {task} ...")
    if result == "unasked":
        print(f"session: note: the task name was not claimed on {remote} ({detail}); starting without a claim", file=sys.stderr)
    return remote if result == "claimed" else ""


def release_claim(primary, branch, remote):
    """Remove the claim this run just made, when the start it was made for did not go on. Fail-open."""
    try:
        load_retirement().drop_claim(primary, branch, remote=remote, env=wip_env())
    except (Exception, SystemExit):
        pass


PERSON_ENV, PERSON_KEY = "SESSION_PERSON", "session.person"
PERSON_SLUG = re.compile(r"^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{0,31}$")
NAME_HINT = (f", or set a person for this clone (`git config {PERSON_KEY} <slug>`, or {PERSON_ENV}=<slug> for one "
             "command) so each person's names differ")


def person_slug(repo):
    """Kit 39: the short person slug this clone's names carry, or "" when none is set (then every name is kit 38's).
    SESSION_PERSON for one command, else `git config session.person` (repo-local or global). Lowercase letters,
    digits and hyphens, at most 32; anything else refuses before a name is made. With a slug, the branch is
    agent/<provider>/<person>/<date>-<task>, the task id <date>-<provider>-<task>@<person>, the worktree
    <repo>-wt-<date>-<task>@<person>, and the backup ref refs/wip/<that branch>. Every parser of these names reads the
    task from the branch's last segment and drops an @<person> suffix, so both shapes are read alike."""
    v = (os.environ.get(PERSON_ENV) or "").strip() or git(repo, "config", "--get", PERSON_KEY, check=False).strip()
    if v and not PERSON_SLUG.match(v):
        sys.exit(f"session: the person slug {v!r} ({PERSON_ENV} or git config {PERSON_KEY}) must be lowercase "
                 "letters, digits and hyphens, at most 32, starting with a letter or digit; nothing was created")
    return v


def cmd_start(a):
    top, primary, linked = here(a.here)
    EVENT["attrs"].update(adopt=bool(a.here), linked=linked)
    if not SLUG.match(a.task):
        sys.exit("session: task must be a short slug of letters, digits, dot, dash, underscore")
    if a.batch is not None and not SLUG.match(a.batch):
        sys.exit("session: --batch must be a short slug of letters, digits, dot, dash, underscore")
    today = dt.date.today().isoformat()
    provider = a.provider or os.environ.get("SESSION_PROVIDER") or "claude"
    base = default_branch(primary)
    git(primary, "fetch", "-q", "origin", base)
    name = a.task if a.task.startswith(today) else f"{today}-{a.task}"
    person = person_slug(primary)  # kit 39: "" unless a person is set, and then every name below is kit 38's
    tid = f"{today}-{provider}-{a.task.removeprefix(today + '-')}" + (f"@{person}" if person else "")
    EVENT["attrs"]["person_set"] = bool(person)
    if a.here:
        # Adopt the worktree we are standing in, normally the Claude desktop app's
        # <repo>/.claude/worktrees/<name>: no second worktree, no new branch, only the task file
        # and handoff that let other sessions see what this one owns (SESSIONS.md rules 2 and 3).
        if not linked:
            sys.exit("session: --here adopts the session worktree you are standing in, and the primary "
                     "checkout is not one; run `session start <task>` without --here to cut a sibling")
        t = my_task(top, f"origin/{base}")
        if t:
            sys.exit(f"session: this worktree already has a task file: {t.relative_to(top)}")
        wt = top
        branch = git(top, "branch", "--show-current")
        if not branch:
            sys.exit("session: --here needs a branch checked out (this worktree is detached)")
        base_commit = git(top, "rev-parse", "--short", git(top, "merge-base", f"origin/{base}", "HEAD"))
        disk_guard(primary, top, None, adopt=True)
    else:
        branch = f"agent/{provider}/{person}/{name}" if person else f"agent/{provider}/{name}"
        wt = primary.parent / (f"{primary.name}-wt-{name}@{person}" if person else f"{primary.name}-wt-{name}")
        if wt.exists():
            sys.exit(f"session: {wt} already exists; pick another task name or `cd` there")
        name_in_use_check(primary, base, branch, tid, a.task)  # kit 39: before anything is created
        base_commit = git(primary, "rev-parse", "--short", f"origin/{base}")
        if a.batch:
            batch_guard(primary, f"origin/{base}", a.batch)  # kit 41: a batch lane is queued above the floor
        disk_guard(primary, wt.parent, f"origin/{base}", adopt=False)
        claimed = claim_at_start(primary, branch, tid, a.task, person)  # kit 40: the last step before anything is created
        try:
            git(primary, "worktree", "add", "-q", "-b", branch, str(wt), f"origin/{base}")
        except SystemExit:
            if claimed:
                release_claim(primary, branch, claimed)
            raise
    activate_prepush(primary)
    write_pin(wt, branch, tid, session_id())  # the commit guard holds this worktree to this branch and session (kit 28)
    try:  # kit 39: this clone's own-work log, so a later start of the same name here is known as this clone's
        load_retirement().own_note(primary, "start", branch=branch, task=tid)
    except (Exception, SystemExit):
        pass
    rel = os.path.relpath(wt, primary)
    (wt / ".agents" / "tasks").mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    (wt / ".agents" / "handoffs").mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    task = {"id": tid, "provider": provider, "status": "active", "branch": branch,
            "worktree": rel, "base_commit": base_commit, "objective": a.objective or "",
            "owned_paths": a.own, "started": dt.datetime.now().astimezone().isoformat(timespec="seconds"),
            "handoff": f".agents/handoffs/{tid}.md"}
    if a.robot:  # a scheduled job's run: the reaper retires it once a later run supersedes it (kit 29)
        task["robot"] = True
    if a.batch:  # kit 41: the batch this lane belongs to, written only when given
        task["batch"] = a.batch
    if person:  # kit 39: written only when set, so a solo record is byte for byte kit 38's
        task["person"] = person
    (wt / ".agents" / "tasks" / f"{tid}.json").write_text(json.dumps(task, indent=2) + "\n")
    tmpl = wt / ".agents" / "HANDOFF.template.md"
    body = tmpl.read_text() if tmpl.exists() else "# Agent handoff: <task>\n"
    fill = {"# Agent handoff: <task>": f"# Agent handoff: {a.task}",
            "- Status: planned | active | review-ready | blocked | complete": "- Status: active",
            "- Provider/agent:": f"- Provider/agent: {provider}",
            "- Objective:": f"- Objective: {a.objective or ''}",
            "- Worktree and branch:": f"- Worktree and branch: `{rel}`, `{branch}`",
            "- Base and head commits:": f"- Base and head commits: base `{base_commit}`; head recorded at merge.",
            "- Owned paths:": "- Owned paths: " + ", ".join(f"`{p}`" for p in a.own)}
    for k, v in fill.items():
        body = body.replace(k, v, 1)
    (wt / ".agents" / "handoffs" / f"{tid}.md").write_text(body)
    print(f"worktree: {wt}\nbranch:   {branch}\ntask:     {tid}\n")
    if a.here:
        print(f"Adopted this worktree. Commit here (specific files), then run:\n  python3 .agents/bin/session land")
    else:
        print(f"Work only under {wt}. Commit there (specific files), then run:\n  python3 .agents/bin/session land")
    print(f"Repos whose pre-commit checks ownership want: export AGENT_NAME={provider}")
    others = [s for s in sessions(primary) if s["path"] != str(wt)]
    if others:
        print(f"\n{len(others)} other live session(s) on this repo; run `python3 .agents/bin/session status` to see them.")


RESUME_SUFFIX = "-cont"


def cmd_resume(a):
    """Kit 40 (M24, M26): continue another clone's unlanded task as a NEW attempt under this clone's own name.

    The task is found on the remote (the same one read `status --all` makes) by its backup ref, its branch, its task
    id or its bare task name. The new attempt gets its own branch (cut from the earlier attempt's backed-up tip, so
    the earlier commits are in it unchanged, with their authors), its own worktree, claim and backup ref, and a task
    record and handoff that cite the attempt they continue: its task id, branch, ref, the commit read and the holder.
    Nothing of the earlier attempt is rewritten or removed: its refs are only read, and its owner's later commits keep
    going to its own ref. The earlier attempt's own task record and handoff file are taken out of the NEW branch in
    the resume commit (the record would otherwise land as a second live owner of the same paths); they stay in the
    earlier attempt's commits and on its ref, and the handoff's text is quoted in the new handoff, so the resumer
    never has to ask the first owner what was going on. No contact with the earlier owner is needed, and no person
    need be set. The new task name is <task>-cont unless --as names another."""
    _, primary, _ = here(a.here)
    today = dt.date.today().isoformat()
    provider = a.provider or os.environ.get("SESSION_PROVIDER") or "claude"
    base = default_branch(primary)
    git(primary, "fetch", "-q", "origin", base)
    rem = remote_work(primary, base)
    if not rem["checked"]:
        sys.exit(f"session: resume reads the task from {rem['remote']}, which could not be asked"
                 + (f" ({rem['error']})" if rem.get("error") else "") + "; nothing was created")

    def short(name):
        return re.sub(r"^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}-", "", name.rsplit("/", 1)[-1])

    want = a.task
    cands = [t for t in rem["tasks"] if want in (t["name"], t["ref"], t["claim_ref"], t["task"],
                                                 t["name"].rsplit("/", 1)[-1], short(t["name"]))]
    if len(cands) != 1:
        listed = "; ".join(f"{t['name']} ({t['holder']})" for t in cands) or "none"
        sys.exit(f"session: resume: {want!r} matched {len(cands)} task(s) on {rem['remote']}: {listed}. Name one by its "
                 "branch or backup ref, as `python3 .agents/bin/session status --all` lists them; nothing was created")
    t = cands[0]
    who = t["holder"] + (f" (person {t['person']})" if t.get("person") else "")
    if t["state"] == "claimed":
        sys.exit(f"session: resume: {t['name']} is claimed by {who} and has nothing saved on {rem['remote']}, so there is "
                 "nothing to continue; start a task of your own under another name. Nothing was created")
    if not t["ahead"]:
        sys.exit(f"session: resume: {t['name']} has nothing ahead of {base} (it landed, or holds no commit yet), so "
                 "there is nothing to continue; nothing was created")
    live = f"branch refs/heads/{t['name']}" in git(primary, "worktree", "list", "--porcelain", env=READ_ONLY).splitlines()
    if t.get("mine") and live:
        sys.exit(f"session: resume: {t['name']} is this clone's own live session; work in its worktree "
                 "(`python3 .agents/bin/session status` names it). Nothing was created")
    tip = t["tip"]
    task = a.as_name or short(t["name"]) + RESUME_SUFFIX
    if not SLUG.match(task):
        sys.exit("session: the new task name must be a short slug of letters, digits, dot, dash, underscore")
    name = task if task.startswith(today) else f"{today}-{task}"
    person = person_slug(primary)
    tid = f"{today}-{provider}-{task.removeprefix(today + '-')}" + (f"@{person}" if person else "")
    branch = f"agent/{provider}/{person}/{name}" if person else f"agent/{provider}/{name}"
    wt = primary.parent / (f"{primary.name}-wt-{name}@{person}" if person else f"{primary.name}-wt-{name}")
    EVENT["attrs"].update(person_set=bool(person), resumed_own=bool(t.get("mine")))
    if wt.exists():
        sys.exit(f"session: {wt} already exists; pick another name with --as")
    if branch == t["name"]:
        sys.exit("session: resume makes a new attempt under a name of its own, and that is the earlier attempt's "
                 "branch; pick another name with --as. Nothing was created")
    name_in_use_check(primary, base, branch, tid, task)
    disk_guard(primary, wt.parent, tip, adopt=False)
    claimed = claim_at_start(primary, branch, tid, task, person)
    try:
        git(primary, "worktree", "add", "-q", "-b", branch, str(wt), tip)
    except SystemExit:
        if claimed:
            release_claim(primary, branch, claimed)
        raise
    activate_prepush(primary)
    write_pin(wt, branch, tid, session_id())
    try:
        load_retirement().own_note(primary, "start", branch=branch, task=tid, continues=t["name"])
    except (Exception, SystemExit):
        pass
    changed = name_list(primary, "--no-renames", f"origin/{base}...{tip}", check=False, env=READ_ONLY)
    was_rel, was = record_at(primary, tip, t["name"], changed)
    was_handoff = was.get("handoff") or ""
    earlier_text = (wt / was_handoff).read_text() if was_handoff and (wt / was_handoff).is_file() else ""
    rel = os.path.relpath(wt, primary)
    base_commit = git(primary, "rev-parse", "--short", git(primary, "merge-base", f"origin/{base}", tip))
    now = dt.datetime.now().astimezone().isoformat(timespec="seconds")
    own = a.own or was.get("owned_paths") or []
    objective = a.objective or was.get("objective") or ""
    rec = {"id": tid, "provider": provider, "status": "active", "branch": branch, "worktree": rel,
           "base_commit": base_commit, "objective": objective, "owned_paths": own, "started": now,
           "handoff": f".agents/handoffs/{tid}.md",
           "continues": {"task": was.get("id") or t["task"], "branch": t["name"], "ref": t["ref"], "commit": tip,
                         "holder": t["holder"], "holder_email": t.get("holder_email", ""),
                         "person": t.get("person", ""), "checkpoint_date": t["date"], "resumed": now}}
    if person:
        rec["person"] = person
    staged = []
    for gone in (was_rel, was_handoff):  # the earlier attempt's own record and handoff: out of the NEW branch only
        if gone and (wt / gone).is_file():
            (wt / gone).unlink()
            staged.append(gone)
    (wt / ".agents" / "tasks").mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    (wt / ".agents" / "handoffs").mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    (wt / ".agents" / "tasks" / f"{tid}.json").write_text(json.dumps(rec, indent=2) + "\n")
    tmpl = wt / ".agents" / "HANDOFF.template.md"
    body = tmpl.read_text() if tmpl.exists() else "# Agent handoff: <task>\n"
    fill = {"# Agent handoff: <task>": f"# Agent handoff: {task}",
            "- Status: planned | active | review-ready | blocked | complete": "- Status: active",
            "- Provider/agent:": f"- Provider/agent: {provider}",
            "- Objective:": f"- Objective: {objective}",
            "- Worktree and branch:": f"- Worktree and branch: `{rel}`, `{branch}`",
            "- Base and head commits:": f"- Base and head commits: base `{base_commit}`; head recorded at merge.",
            "- Owned paths:": "- Owned paths: " + ", ".join(f"`{x}`" for x in own)}
    for k, v in fill.items():
        body = body.replace(k, v, 1)
    body = body.rstrip("\n") + (
        f"\n\n## Continues an earlier attempt\n\nThis task is a new attempt that continues `{rec['continues']['task'] or t['name']}` "
        f"(branch `{t['name']}`), read from `{t['ref']}` at commit `{tip[:12]}` by {who}, dated {t['date']}. The "
        "earlier attempt is unchanged by this one: its ref, its commits and their authors stay as they are, and "
        "anything it produces later stays on its own ref until its owner and this one agree what lands.\n")
    if earlier_text:
        body += ("\nThe earlier attempt's handoff as of that commit, quoted:\n\n"
                 + "\n".join(("> " + l).rstrip() for l in earlier_text.splitlines()) + "\n")
    (wt / ".agents" / "handoffs" / f"{tid}.md").write_text(body)
    staged += [f".agents/tasks/{tid}.json", f".agents/handoffs/{tid}.md"]
    git(wt, "add", "--", *staged)
    commit(wt, provider, "-q", "-m", f"session: resume {rec['continues']['task'] or t['name']} as {tid}\n\n"
           f"A new attempt that continues {t['name']} from {t['ref']} at {tip[:12]} (by {t['holder']}). The earlier "
           "attempt's ref and commits are untouched.")
    EVENT["attrs"]["resumed_ahead"] = t["ahead"]
    print(f"worktree: {wt}\nbranch:   {branch}\ntask:     {tid}\n")
    print(f"A new attempt, continuing {t['name']} at {tip[:10]} by {who}, {load_retirement().age_text(t['date'])}"
          + (" (a snapshot that includes uncommitted files)" if t.get("snapshot") else "") + ".\n"
          f"Its {t['ahead']} commit(s) are in this branch with their authors. The earlier attempt is untouched: its ref "
          f"{t['ref']} stays its owner's.\nWork only under {wt}. Commit there (specific files), then run:\n"
          "  python3 .agents/bin/session land")
    print(f"Repos whose pre-commit checks ownership want: export AGENT_NAME={provider}")
    return 0


def name_list(cwd, *a, check=True, env=None):
    """`git diff --name-only` read with -z: unstripped and unquoted. Without -z git prints a path with
    a non-ASCII byte as a quoted escape (core.quotePath, on by default), which matches no ask path
    and, passed back as a pathspec, no file."""
    return [x for x in run(["git", "diff", "--name-only", "-z", *a], cwd, check, env).stdout.split("\0") if x]


def changed_paths(wt, base):
    """Paths a worktree changes: committed since the base plus uncommitted. Both are read with -z,
    unstripped and unquoted: `git()` strips its output, which ate the leading space of the first
    porcelain line (` M path`) and so the path's first character (an engagement repo, 2026-09-25), and without
    -z git quotes a path with a space or a non-ASCII byte. A rename gives both its old and new path,
    because another session touching either one is an overlap. wt is usually another session's worktree:
    both reads run READ_ONLY, so the status never takes its index lock (kit 20)."""
    committed = name_list(wt, "--no-renames", f"origin/{base}...HEAD", check=False, env=READ_ONLY)
    fields = run(["git", "status", "--porcelain=v1", "-z", "--untracked-files=all"], wt, check=False,
                 env=READ_ONLY).stdout.split("\0")
    dirty = []
    while fields:
        e = fields.pop(0)
        if len(e) > 3:
            dirty.append(e[3:])
            if {"R", "C"} & set(e[:2]) and fields:
                dirty.append(fields.pop(0))
    return sorted(set(committed + dirty)), dirty


def sessions(primary):
    """Every live session worktree of the repository, read without taking any of their locks (kit 20)."""
    base = default_branch(primary)
    out, cur = [], {}
    for line in git(primary, "worktree", "list", "--porcelain", env=READ_ONLY).splitlines() + [""]:
        if not line:
            if cur:
                out.append(cur)
            cur = {}
            continue
        k, _, v = line.partition(" ")
        cur[k] = v or True
    res = []
    for w in out:
        p = Path(w["worktree"])
        if p.resolve() == primary.resolve() or w.get("prunable") or not p.exists():
            continue
        br = str(w.get("branch", "")).removeprefix("refs/heads/") or "(detached)"
        changed, dirty = changed_paths(p, base)
        counts = git(p, "rev-list", "--left-right", "--count", f"origin/{base}...HEAD", check=False, env=READ_ONLY).split()
        t = my_task(p, f"origin/{base}")
        task = json.loads(t.read_text()) if t and t.exists() else {}
        res.append({"path": str(p), "branch": br, "task": task.get("id", ""), "status": task.get("status", ""),
                    "objective": task.get("objective", ""), "owned_paths": task.get("owned_paths", []),
                    "changed": changed, "uncommitted": len(dirty),
                    "behind": int(counts[0]) if counts else 0, "ahead": int(counts[1]) if len(counts) > 1 else 0,
                    **({"land_refused": task["land_refused"]} if task.get("status") == "blocked"
                       and isinstance(task.get("land_refused"), dict) else {})})
    return res


def overlaps(ss):
    found = []
    for i, l in enumerate(ss):
        for r in ss[i + 1:]:
            ignore = lambda x: x.startswith(".agents/tasks/") or x.startswith(".agents/handoffs/")
            files = sorted(set(x for x in l["changed"] if not ignore(x)) & set(x for x in r["changed"] if not ignore(x)))
            owned = [(lp, rp) for lp in l["owned_paths"] for rp in r["owned_paths"]
                     if lp == rp or lp.startswith(rp.rstrip("/") + "/") or rp.startswith(lp.rstrip("/") + "/")]
            if files or owned:
                found.append({"left": l["branch"], "right": r["branch"], "files": files, "owned": owned})
    return found


def span(h):
    """Hours as a person reads them: minutes under an hour, days past two."""
    return f"{round(h * 60)} min" if h < 1 else f"{h:.1f} h" if h < 48 else f"{h / 24:.1f} d"


def backup_line(b):
    """One line on a session's backup for `status` (kit 35), from retirement.backup_summary."""
    age = f"newest good copy {span(b['age_h'])} old" if b.get("age_h") is not None else "no good copy recorded"
    who = {"commit": "the commit hook", "reaper": "the hourly reaper"}.get(b.get("writer"), "")
    if b["state"] == "current":
        local = ("; its uncommitted files stay on this machine (a public repository gets the committed tip only)"
                 if "uncommitted kept local" in (b.get("detail") or "") else "")
        where = "the private backup remote" if b.get("result") == "pushed-private" or \
            "on the private backup remote" in (b.get("detail") or "") else "origin"
        return (f"backup: current on {where} ({b.get('ref') or 'refs/wip'}), {age}" + (f", by {who}" if who else "")
                + (", checked on the remote just now" if b.get("checked") else "") + local)
    if b["state"] == "not-checked":  # kit 39
        return (f"backup: NOT CHECKED: the remote could not be asked in this call, so nothing here proves a copy is "
                f"there now; the record on this machine says {age}")
    if b["state"] == "missing":
        return (f"backup: MISSING: {b.get('ref') or 'refs/wip'} is not on the remote although a copy was recorded "
                f"({age}); this work is on this machine only")
    if b["state"] == "overwritten":
        return f"backup: OVERWRITTEN: {b.get('detail') or 'the remote ref holds a commit this clone did not write'}; this work is on this machine only"
    if b["state"] == "failing":
        since = f" for {span(b['failing_h'])}" if b.get("failing_h") is not None else ""
        why = f" ({b['result']}: {b['detail'][:120]})" if b.get("detail") else f" ({b['result']})"
        return f"backup: FAILING{since}{why}; this work is on this machine only; {age}"
    if b["state"] == "behind":
        return (f"backup: BEHIND: HEAD has commits no backup holds; {age}. A commit made with SESSION_WIP_BACKUP=0, by "
                "a land's own step, or on a branch outside agent/ and claude/ pushes none; the hourly reaper catches up")
    if b["state"] == "local":
        return (f"backup: KEPT LOCAL: origin is public or its visibility could not be told, so the commit hook does not "
                f"push unlanded work there (kit 37) and neither does the hourly reaper (kit 38); this work is on this machine only, not remotely durable; {age}. "
                "Name a private remote as private_backup_remote in .agents/repository-policy.json to back it up")
    return "backup: none recorded on this machine yet"


def remote_reading(rt, s, p, gd, head, remote, heads):
    """Kit 39: the backup reading is judged against the remote ref read in this call, never only the local record,
    which kept saying "current" after another clone's push had replaced the ref (the 2026-11-17 two-person trial).
    current: the remote ref holds HEAD (or this clone's snapshot on top of it, or HEAD is on an origin branch);
    not-checked: the remote could not be asked in this call; missing: the ref is not on the remote although a good copy
    was recorded; overwritten: the ref holds a commit this clone did not write; behind: an older commit of this clone.
    A reading of "local" (kit 37: nothing is pushed for this origin) is left as it is."""
    b = s.get("backup") or {}
    if b.get("state") == "local" or not head:
        return
    name = s.get("stopped_land") or (s["branch"] if s.get("branch") and s["branch"] != "(detached)" else "")
    if not name:
        return
    ref = rt.WIP_NS + name
    b["checked"], b["ref"] = heads is not None, b.get("ref") or ref
    if heads is None:
        if b.get("state") == "current":
            b["state"] = "not-checked"
        return
    have = heads.get(name, "")
    b["remote_tip"] = have
    if have == head:
        b["state"] = "current"
        return
    if have:
        line = git(p, "log", "-1", "--format=%P", have, check=False, env=READ_ONLY).split()
        if line and line[0] == head and rt.own_pushed(p, ref, have, (gd,)):
            b["state"] = "current"  # this clone's snapshot of HEAD plus its uncommitted files
        elif rt.sha_owned(p, ref, have, name, (head,), (gd,)):
            b["state"] = "behind"
        else:
            if not rt.has_commit(p, have):
                rt.fetch_tip(p, ref, remote)
            b["state"], b["detail"] = "overwritten", rt.foreign_text(p, ref, have, remote)
        return
    if git(p, "branch", "-r", "--contains", head, check=False, env=READ_ONLY):
        if b.get("state") != "none":
            b["state"] = "current"  # nothing unlanded: HEAD is on an origin branch
    elif b.get("state") in ("current", "behind") and b.get("age_h") is not None:
        b["state"] = "missing"


def backup_readings(ss):
    """Add each session's backup reading and stopped land to the status records (kit 35). Fail-open: a kit without
    retirement.py, or a reading that raises, leaves the session as it was and says nothing."""
    try:
        rt = load_retirement()
    except (Exception, SystemExit):
        return
    heads, remote = None, None
    try:  # kit 39: one read of the backup refs on the destination per call; None when it cannot be asked
        if ss:
            remote, _ = rt.backup_destination(Path(ss[0]["path"]))
            heads = rt.wip_heads(Path(ss[0]["path"]), remote=remote) if remote else None
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - fail-open: the reading then says "not checked"
        heads = None
    for s in ss:
        try:
            p = Path(s["path"])
            gd = git(p, "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-dir", check=False, env=READ_ONLY)
            head = git(p, "rev-parse", "-q", "--verify", "HEAD", check=False, env=READ_ONLY)
            s["backup"] = rt.backup_summary(gd, head)
            s["stopped_land"] = rt.stopped_rebase_branch(gd) if gd else ""
        except Exception:
            continue
        try:
            remote_reading(rt, s, p, gd, head, remote, heads)
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - a reading that could not be checked never says "current"
            if (s.get("backup") or {}).get("state") == "current":
                s["backup"]["state"] = "not-checked"
    for k in ("current", "behind", "failing", "local", "none", "not-checked", "missing", "overwritten"):
        EVENT["attrs"][f"backup_{k.replace('-', '_')}"] = sum(1 for s in ss if (s.get("backup") or {}).get("state") == k)
    EVENT["attrs"]["stopped_lands"] = sum(1 for s in ss if s.get("stopped_land"))


SEEN_NS = "refs/session-seen/"  # this clone's read-only copy of the remote's hidden refs (kit 40): never pushed


def remote_work(primary, base):
    """Kit 40 (M23): every task the remote holds as a claim (refs/claims/) or a backup (refs/wip/), whoever wrote it:
    its holder (the author of the backup's tip, or of the claim; the person slug when the task record, the claim or the
    branch name carries one), its age, and whether it is ahead of the base branch. Before this the only way to see
    another clone's open work was `git ls-remote origin 'refs/wip/*'` by hand (the 2026-11-17 two-person trial, P3).

    Read-only: one fetch of the hidden refs into refs/session-seen/ in this clone (objects and a local copy of the ref
    names, pruned to what the remote holds now), then local reads. Nothing is pushed, no branch or worktree changes,
    and no person need be set. Returns {"checked": False, ...} when the remote cannot be asked: nothing is then said
    about what others hold. The names are the commits' own git config identities: supplied, not verified."""
    out = {"checked": False, "remote": "origin", "tasks": []}
    try:
        rt = load_retirement()
        remote = rt.backup_destination(primary)[0] or "origin"
        out["remote"] = remote
        f = run(["git", "fetch", "-q", "--prune", "--no-write-fetch-head", remote, f"+{rt.WIP_NS}*:{SEEN_NS}wip/*",
                 f"+{rt.CLAIM_NS}*:{SEEN_NS}claims/*"], primary, check=False, env=wip_env())
        if f.returncode:
            out["error"] = " ".join((f.stderr or f.stdout).split())[:200]
            return out
        seen = {"wip": {}, "claims": {}}
        fmt = "%(refname)%1f%(objectname)%1f%(authorname)%1f%(authoremail)%1f%(committerdate:iso-strict)%1f%(subject)"
        for line in git(primary, "for-each-ref", f"--format={fmt}", SEEN_NS, check=False, env=READ_ONLY).splitlines():
            ref, sha, who, mail, when, subject = (line.split("\x1f") + [""] * 6)[:6]
            kind, _, name = ref[len(SEEN_NS):].partition("/")
            if kind in seen and name:
                seen[kind][name] = {"sha": sha, "who": who or "an unknown author", "mail": mail.strip("<>"), "when": when, "subject": subject}
        for name in sorted(set(seen["wip"]) | set(seen["claims"])):
            w, c = seen["wip"].get(name), seen["claims"].get(name)
            info = rt.claim_info(primary, c["sha"]) if c else {}
            t = {"name": name, "state": "backed-up" if w else "claimed", "ref": rt.WIP_NS + name if w else "",
                 "claim_ref": rt.CLAIM_NS + name if c else "", "tip": (w or c)["sha"], "holder": (w or c)["who"],
                 "holder_email": (w or c)["mail"], "date": (w or c)["when"], "task": info.get("task", ""),
                 "person": info.get("person", ""), "objective": "", "ahead": 0, "behind": 0, "files": 0,
                 "snapshot": bool(w and w["subject"].startswith("wip snapshot"))}
            try:
                t["age_s"] = max(0, round(time.time() - dt.datetime.fromisoformat(t["date"]).timestamp()))
            except (TypeError, ValueError):
                t["age_s"] = None
            if w:
                counts = git(primary, "rev-list", "--left-right", "--count", f"origin/{base}...{w['sha']}", check=False,
                             env=READ_ONLY).split()
                if len(counts) == 2:
                    t["behind"], t["ahead"] = int(counts[0]), int(counts[1])
                changed = name_list(primary, "--no-renames", f"origin/{base}...{w['sha']}", check=False, env=READ_ONLY)
                t["files"] = sum(1 for x in changed if not x.startswith((".agents/tasks/", ".agents/handoffs/")))
                _, rec = record_at(primary, w["sha"], name, changed)
                if rec:
                    t.update(task=rec.get("id") or t["task"], person=rec.get("person") or t["person"],
                             objective=rec.get("objective") or "", task_status=rec.get("status") or "")
                    if isinstance(rec.get("continues"), dict):
                        t["continues"] = rec["continues"].get("branch") or rec["continues"].get("task") or ""
                    if rec.get("status") == "blocked" and isinstance(rec.get("land_refused"), dict):
                        t["land_refused"] = rec["land_refused"]
                t["mine"] = bool(rt.sha_owned(primary, rt.WIP_NS + name, w["sha"], name))
            else:
                t["mine"] = bool(rt.own_claimed(primary, rt.CLAIM_NS + name, c["sha"]))
            parts = name.split("/")
            if not t["person"] and len(parts) == 4 and parts[0] == "agent":
                t["person"] = parts[2]  # agent/<provider>/<person>/<date>-<task> (kit 39)
            out["tasks"].append(t)
        out["checked"] = True
    except (Exception, SystemExit) as e:  # noqa: BLE001 - a view that cannot be read says so, and status goes on
        out["error"] = f"{type(e).__name__}: {e}"[:200]
    EVENT["attrs"].update(remote_checked=out["checked"], remote_tasks=len(out["tasks"]),
                          remote_others=sum(1 for t in out["tasks"] if not t.get("mine")))
    return out


def record_at(primary, sha, name, changed):
    """(path, record) of the task record that names branch `name` in commit `sha`, among the paths that commit
    changes against the base (`changed`); ("", {}) when it carries none (a record is committed with the first
    commit that adds it, by a snapshot, or by land)."""
    for rel in (x for x in changed if x.startswith(".agents/tasks/") and x.endswith(".json")):
        try:
            rec = json.loads(git(primary, "show", f"{sha}:{rel}", check=False, env=READ_ONLY))
        except ValueError:
            continue
        if isinstance(rec, dict) and rec.get("branch") == name:
            return rel, rec
    return "", {}


def refused_line(lr):
    """One line on a land refused against someone else's landing (kit 40), from the task record's land_refused."""
    who = "; ".join(f"{x.get('commit', '')[:10]} by {x.get('author', 'an unknown author')}" for x in lr.get("landings") or [])
    return (f"LAND REFUSED {lr.get('at', '')}: it changes the same lines as {who or 'another landing'} "
            f"({', '.join((lr.get('files') or [])[:5])}). The two owners decide; no agent chooses. Then: "
            "`session land --owners-decided \"<who decided, and what>\"`")


def remote_lines(rem, base):
    """The remote view of `status --all` as text: other clones' open work first, then this clone's own."""
    if not rem["checked"]:
        return [f"\nremote: NOT CHECKED: {rem['remote']} could not be asked in this call"
                + (f" ({rem['error']})" if rem.get("error") else "") + ", so nothing here says what other clones hold"]
    rt = load_retirement()
    others = [t for t in rem["tasks"] if not t.get("mine")]
    mine = [t for t in rem["tasks"] if t.get("mine")]
    L = [f"\nremote: open work on {rem['remote']}, read just now: {len(others)} from other clone(s), {len(mine)} from this "
         "clone. A holder is the name on the commit: supplied, not verified"]
    for label, rows in (("other clones", others), ("this clone", mine)):
        if rows:
            L.append(f"\n{label}:")
        for t in rows:
            who = t["holder"] + (f" (person {t['person']})" if t.get("person") else "")
            L.append(f"- {t['name']}  ({t['task'] or 'no task record saved'})")
            if t["state"] == "claimed":
                L.append(f"  CLAIMED, nothing saved yet: holder {who}, claim {rt.age_text(t['date'])}")
            else:
                ahead = (f"{t['ahead']} ahead of {base}, {t['behind']} behind; {t['files']} file(s)" if t["ahead"] else
                         f"nothing ahead of {base} (landed, or not begun)")
                L.append(f"  BACKED UP: holder {who}, tip {t['tip'][:10]} {rt.age_text(t['date'])}"
                         + (" (a snapshot that includes uncommitted files)" if t.get("snapshot") else "") + f"; {ahead}")
            if t.get("land_refused"):
                L.append(f"  {refused_line(t['land_refused'])}")
            if t.get("continues"):
                L.append(f"  continues an earlier attempt: {t['continues']}")
            if t.get("objective"):
                L.append(f"  objective: {t['objective']}")
            if label == "other clones" and t["state"] == "backed-up" and t["ahead"]:
                L.append(f"  continue it as a new attempt of your own: python3 .agents/bin/session resume {t['name']}")
    if not rem["tasks"]:
        L.append("no claimed or backed-up task on the remote")
    return L


def cmd_status(a):
    _, primary, _ = here(a.here)
    base = default_branch(primary)
    run(["git", "fetch", "-q", "origin", base], primary, check=False)
    ss = sessions(primary)
    ov = overlaps(ss)
    EVENT["attrs"].update(sessions=len(ss), overlaps=len(ov))
    backup_readings(ss)
    rem = remote_work(primary, base) if getattr(a, "all", False) else None  # kit 40
    off = os.environ.get(WIP_SWITCH, "").strip() == "0"
    EVENT["attrs"]["backup_off"] = off
    pbranch = git(primary, "branch", "--show-current", check=False) or "(detached)"
    pdirty = len(git(primary, "status", "--porcelain", check=False, env=READ_ONLY).splitlines())
    pstaged = name_list(primary, "--cached", check=False, env=READ_ONLY)  # a stale index, named (kit 28)
    pbehind = primary_behind(primary, base)
    if a.json:
        print(json.dumps({"primary": str(primary), "primary_branch": pbranch, "primary_dirty": pdirty,
                          "primary_staged": pstaged, "primary_behind": pbehind, "backup_off_in_shell": off,
                          "sessions": ss, "overlaps": ov, **({"remote": rem} if rem is not None else {})}, indent=2))
        return 1 if ov else 0
    if off:
        print(f"backups: OFF in this shell ({WIP_SWITCH}=0 is set): commits made from it push no backup to origin. "
              "Unset it unless a kit step set it for itself")
    print(f"primary: {primary}  on {pbranch}, {pdirty} uncommitted path(s)" +
          ("" if pbranch == base else f"  WARNING: primary should be on {base}"))
    if pstaged:
        print(f"primary: WARNING: {len(pstaged)} path(s) STAGED and not committed {names(pstaged)}: a stale index; "
              f"a commit here would sweep them in (the commit guard refuses one; the session that staged them should "
              f"`git restore --staged` or stash them by tag)")
    print(f"primary: {pbehind} behind origin/{base}" +
          ("" if pbehind == 0 else "  (run `session finish` for a landed session, or wait for the hub's primary-ff sweep)"))
    if not ss:
        print("no live sessions")
    for s in ss:
        print(f"\n- {s['branch']}  ({s['task'] or 'no task file'})\n  {s['path']}")
        if s.get("stopped_land"):
            print(f"  LAND STOPPED at a rebase conflict on {s['stopped_land']}: resolve by meaning, `git add` the files, "
                  "`git rebase --continue`, then `session land` again")
        if s.get("land_refused"):
            print(f"  {refused_line(s['land_refused'])}")
        if s["objective"]:
            print(f"  objective: {s['objective']}")
        print(f"  {len(s['changed'])} changed file(s), {s['uncommitted']} uncommitted; {s['ahead']} ahead, {s['behind']} behind {base}")
        if s.get("backup"):
            print(f"  {backup_line(s['backup'])}")
    for o in ov:
        print(f"\nOVERLAP {o['left']} and {o['right']}: files {o['files'][:10]} owned {o['owned'][:5]}")
    if rem is not None:
        print("\n".join(remote_lines(rem, base)))
    return 1 if ov else 0


def ask_hits(changed, pol):
    pats = pol.get("ask_paths", [])
    return [x for x in changed for p in pats if fnmatch.fnmatch(x, p) or x == p.rstrip("/") or x.startswith(p.rstrip("/") + "/")]


def requires_pr(top, base):
    """Why the base branch must be reached through a PR, or "" when a direct push is allowed.

    A ruleset rule of type pull_request or required_status_checks, or classic branch protection
    that requires reviews or status checks. When GitHub cannot be asked, returns "" and the
    push itself is the test: a rejected protected-branch push falls back to a PR."""
    R = gh_repo(top)
    if not R:
        return ""
    r = run(["gh", "api", f"repos/{R[1]}/rules/branches/{base}"], top, check=False)
    if r.returncode == 0:
        try:
            types = {x.get("type") for x in json.loads(r.stdout or "[]")}
        except ValueError:
            types = set()
        hit = sorted(types & {"pull_request", "required_status_checks"})
        if hit:
            return f"a ruleset on {base} requires {' and '.join(hit)}"
    p = run(["gh", "api", f"repos/{R[1]}/branches/{base}/protection"], top, check=False)
    if p.returncode == 0:
        try:
            prot = json.loads(p.stdout or "{}")
        except ValueError:
            prot = {}
        if prot.get("required_pull_request_reviews") or prot.get("required_status_checks"):
            return f"branch protection on {base} requires reviews or status checks"
    return ""


def land_message(top, base, title, branch, since=None):
    """Squash message for a local landing: the session's subject, each commit's subject, and the
    commits' trailers (Co-Authored-By and the like) carried over once each."""
    since = since or f"origin/{base}"
    subjects = [x for x in git(top, "log", "--reverse", "--format=%s", f"{since}..HEAD").splitlines()
                if not x.startswith("session: close")]
    subject = title or (subjects[0] if subjects else f"{branch}: land")
    trailers = []
    for l in git(top, "log", "--reverse", "--format=%(trailers:only,unfold)", f"{since}..HEAD").splitlines():
        if l.strip() and l not in trailers:
            trailers.append(l)
    # Kit 39: a combined landing names every other author whose commits it squashes, so no one's contribution
    # collapses into the lander's identity (the 2026-11-17 two-person trial, P6). The names are the commits' own
    # git config identities: supplied, not verified.
    me = git(top, "var", "GIT_AUTHOR_IDENT", check=False).rsplit(">", 1)[0] + ">"
    have = {l.lower() for l in trailers}
    for who in git(top, "log", "--reverse", "--format=%an <%ae>", f"{since}..HEAD").splitlines():
        line = f"Co-authored-by: {who}"
        if who.strip() and who != me and line.lower() not in have:
            trailers.append(line)
            have.add(line.lower())
    body = "\n".join(f"- {x}" for x in subjects) if len(subjects) > 1 else ""
    note = f"Landed locally by session land from {branch} (WS-DDR-143 amendment 2026-09-24)."
    return "\n\n".join(x for x in (subject, body, note, "\n".join(trailers)) if x)


def foreign_paths(top, squash, parent, own_base, head="HEAD"):
    """Paths the squash changes against the commit it lands on that none of the branch's own commits
    (own_base..head) touched. A correct squash has none: its tree is the branch rebased onto parent.
    Anything listed would be a silent revert of another session's landing (kit 19)."""
    landing = set(name_list(top, "--no-renames", parent, squash))
    own = set(x for x in run(["git", "log", "--no-renames", "-m", "--name-only", "-z", "--format=", f"{own_base}..{head}"],
                             top, check=False).stdout.replace("\n", "\0").split("\0") if x)
    return sorted(landing - own)


# KF-11, the knowledge farm's backpressure rule (kit 23): any content change over 25 files, counted per session
# and per day, lands only as a reviewed diff. On 2026-10-02 agents rewrote about 1,450 private files in place
# across many repos and landed them the same day, unread. The rule is measured on totals, so splitting the work
# into small lands does not dodge it. Advisory for now: `land` prints the advisory, writes a per-file diff
# summary into the session's handoff (which becomes the PR description on the PR route), and tells Witness.
# SESSION_KF11_ENFORCE=1, or "kf11": {"enforce": true} in .agents/repository-policy.json, turns it into a
# refusal of every route but `--no-merge` (a PR left open for a human). The rule text, under the Workspaces root:
KF11_RULE = "Core/products/_intake/2026-10-03-knowledge-farm-projection/rules/knowledge-farm-rules.md"
KF11_THRESHOLD = 25
# What is not content: the kit's own session records, lockfiles and generated outputs. Generated outputs are the
# repository's generated_patterns and kf11.exclude (repository-policy.json), files marked linguist-generated in
# .gitattributes, and the defaults below. Of what is left, only prose and data count (kit 34): code, images and
# binaries do not, and neither does JSON or YAML that a program wrote (under a fixtures folder, named .gen. or
# .generated., or naming its generator in its first kilobyte). In its first four weeks the count fired five times
# in one product repo, every time on new code and test fixtures, never on the rewritten words the rule is about.
# kf11.include (globs) counts more paths as prose, for a repository whose words live in another format. A deletion
# of a prose or data file counts.
KF11_NOT_CONTENT_DIRS = (".agents/",)
KF11_PROSE_EXT = {".md", ".markdown", ".mdx", ".txt", ".text", ".rst", ".adoc", ".asciidoc", ".org"}
KF11_DATA_EXT = {".yaml", ".yml", ".json", ".jsonl", ".ndjson", ".csv", ".tsv"}
KF11_PROSE_NAMES = {"readme", "changelog", "license", "notice", "authors", "contributing", "copying"}
KF11_FIXTURE_DIRS = {"fixtures", "__fixtures__"}
KF11_MACHINE_DATA_EXT = {".yaml", ".yml", ".json", ".jsonl", ".ndjson"}
# a generator's stamp: a top-level generated_by or generated_from key, or a "generated by", "@generated" or
# "DO NOT EDIT" comment line (a bare "Generated: <date>" note does not count: agents write those by hand)
KF11_STAMP = re.compile(rb"^(?:[ \t]{0,2}[\"']?generated_(?:by|from)[\"']?[ \t]*:"
                        rb"|[ \t]*(?:#|//)[ \t]*(?:@generated\b|(?:auto-?)?generated[ \t]+(?:by|from)\b|do not edit\b))",
                        re.I | re.M)
KF11_STAMP_BYTES = 1024
KF11_LOCKFILES = {"package-lock.json", "npm-shrinkwrap.json", "yarn.lock", "pnpm-lock.yaml", "bun.lockb", "bun.lock",
                  "poetry.lock", "Pipfile.lock", "uv.lock", "pdm.lock", "Cargo.lock", "Gemfile.lock", "composer.lock",
                  "go.sum", "flake.lock", "mix.lock", "Podfile.lock", "packages.lock.json"}
KF11_GENERATED = ["**/node_modules/**", "**/__pycache__/**", "**/.venv/**", "**/dist/**", "**/generated/**",
                  "*.pyc", "*.min.js", "*.min.css", "*.js.map", "*.css.map", ".intent/observability/**"]
KF11_START, KF11_END = "<!-- kf11:start -->", "<!-- kf11:end -->"
KF11_ROWS = 300  # rows of the per-file table in the handoff; the whole diff is one git command away
# The reviewer's answer (kit 34): one line in the review block, blank until `land --kf11-verdict yes|no
# --kf11-reviewer NAME` writes who said what and when. A no refuses the land; under enforcement a yes lands it
# through a PR, the route the rule names. Before kit 34 the block asked for a yes or no and had nowhere to keep it.
KF11_VERDICT_LINE = re.compile(r"^- Reviewer verdict: (.*?)\s*$", re.M)
KF11_VERDICT_SET = re.compile(r"^(yes|no), by (.+), at (\S+)$")
KF11_VERDICT_BLANK = "(blank)"
LANDED_FROM = re.compile(r"Landed locally by session land from (\S+) \(")


def _glob(path, pattern):
    """The audit's matcher: fnmatch, with a leading **/ also matching at the top level."""
    return fnmatch.fnmatch(path, pattern) or fnmatch.fnmatch(path, pattern.removeprefix("**/"))


def _kf11_ext(path):
    name = path.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]
    stem, dot, ext = name.rpartition(".")
    return ("." + ext.lower()) if dot and stem else "", name


def _kf11_machine_data(top, path, ext):
    """JSON or YAML a program wrote (kit 34): under a fixtures folder, named .gen. or .generated., or carrying a
    generator's stamp in its first kilobyte. A file that cannot be read is not judged machine-written, so it counts."""
    if ext not in KF11_MACHINE_DATA_EXT:
        return False
    parts = path.split("/")
    if any(x in KF11_FIXTURE_DIRS for x in parts[:-1]):
        return True
    name = parts[-1].lower()
    if ".gen." in name or ".generated." in name:
        return True
    try:
        f = top / path
        if f.is_symlink() or not f.is_file():
            return False
        with open(f, "rb") as fh:
            return bool(KF11_STAMP.search(fh.read(KF11_STAMP_BYTES)))
    except Exception:
        return False


def kf11_classify(top, paths):
    """(content, narrowed): the paths that are content (see KF11_NOT_CONTENT_DIRS above), and how many of the rest
    were left out only because they are code or machine-written data (kit 34). An unreadable policy or a failed
    attribute read only means fewer exclusions, so the count errs high, never low."""
    pol = policy(top)
    kf = pol.get("kf11") if isinstance(pol.get("kf11"), dict) else {}
    pats = [p for p in KF11_GENERATED + list(pol.get("generated_patterns", [])) + list(kf.get("exclude", []))
            if isinstance(p, str)]
    include = [p for p in (kf.get("include") if isinstance(kf.get("include"), list) else []) if isinstance(p, str)]
    out = [p for p in paths if not p.startswith(KF11_NOT_CONTENT_DIRS) and p.rsplit("/", 1)[-1] not in KF11_LOCKFILES
           and not any(_glob(p, x) for x in pats)]
    if out:
        r = subprocess.run(["git", "check-attr", "-z", "--stdin", "linguist-generated"], cwd=top, capture_output=True,
                           input="\0".join(out).encode() + b"\0", env={**os.environ, **READ_ONLY})
        if r.returncode == 0:
            f = r.stdout.split(b"\0")
            gen = {f[i].decode(errors="replace") for i in range(0, len(f) - 2, 3) if f[i + 2] in (b"set", b"true")}
            out = [p for p in out if p not in gen]
    kept = []
    for p in out:
        ext, name = _kf11_ext(p)
        words = (ext in KF11_PROSE_EXT or ext in KF11_DATA_EXT or (not ext and name.lower() in KF11_PROSE_NAMES)
                 or any(_glob(p, x) for x in include))
        if words and not _kf11_machine_data(top, p, ext):
            kept.append(p)
    return kept, len(out) - len(kept)


def kf11_content(top, paths):
    """The paths that are content: prose and data, never code or machine-written data (kit 34)."""
    return kf11_classify(top, paths)[0]


def kf11_read_verdict(text):
    """The reviewer's answer in a handoff's KF-11 block: {"verdict": "yes"|"no", "reviewer", "at"}, {"verdict": None}
    while the line is blank, or None when there is no block or the block predates the line (kit 34)."""
    i, j = text.find(KF11_START), text.find(KF11_END)
    if i < 0 or j < i:
        return None
    m = KF11_VERDICT_LINE.search(text[i:j])
    if not m:
        return None
    s = KF11_VERDICT_SET.match(m.group(1))
    return {"verdict": s.group(1), "reviewer": s.group(2), "at": s.group(3)} if s else {"verdict": None}


def _kf11_verdict_text(verdict):
    if not verdict:
        return KF11_VERDICT_BLANK
    return f"{verdict['verdict']}, by {verdict['reviewer']}, at {verdict['at']}"


def _branch_agent(branch):
    """The agent a branch name carries: agent/<provider>/<task>, or the desktop app's <provider>/<name>."""
    parts = (branch or "").split("/")
    return (parts[1] if parts[0] == "agent" and len(parts) > 2 else parts[0]) or "unknown"


def kf11_agent(task, branch):
    """Who is landing: AGENT_NAME, else the task's provider, else the branch's (agent/<provider>/..., or the
    desktop app's <provider>/<name>)."""
    if os.environ.get("AGENT_NAME", "").strip():
        return os.environ["AGENT_NAME"].strip()
    return task.get("provider") or _branch_agent(branch)


def kf11_ledger(primary):
    """This clone's record of landings, in the common git dir every worktree shares (never committed)."""
    return Path(git(primary, "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir")) / "session-kf11.jsonl"


def kf11_earlier(top, primary, pin, branch, agent, day_start):
    """(this session's earlier landed content files, today's landed content files by this agent), as sets.
    Read from origin/<base> (a local landing's squash message names its branch, so landings made on any machine
    count) and from this clone's ledger (which also holds landings that went through a PR). Fail-open: a read
    that breaks leaves what was read so far."""
    mine, today = set(), set()
    try:
        log = run(["git", "log", "-F", "--grep=Landed locally by session land from ", "--format=%H%x1f%cI%x1f%B%x1e",
                   pin], top, check=False, env=READ_ONLY).stdout
        for rec in log.split("\x1e"):
            parts = rec.strip("\n").split("\x1f")
            m = LANDED_FROM.search(parts[2]) if len(parts) == 3 else None
            if not m:
                continue
            ours = m.group(1) == branch
            todays = dt.datetime.fromisoformat(parts[1]) >= day_start and _branch_agent(m.group(1)) == agent
            if not (ours or todays):
                continue
            names_ = [x for x in run(["git", "diff-tree", "--no-commit-id", "-r", "-z", "--name-only", "--no-renames",
                                      parts[0].strip()], top, check=False, env=READ_ONLY).stdout.split("\0") if x]
            files = set(kf11_content(top, names_))
            if ours:
                mine |= files
            if todays:
                today |= files
        led = kf11_ledger(primary)
        for line in led.read_text().splitlines() if led.is_file() else []:
            try:
                e = json.loads(line)
            except ValueError:
                continue
            files = {x for x in e.get("files", []) if isinstance(x, str)}
            if e.get("branch") == branch:
                mine |= files
            if e.get("agent") == agent and e.get("day") == day_start.date().isoformat():
                today |= files
    except Exception:
        pass
    return mine, today


def kf11_record(primary, agent, branch, files):
    """One line per landing in this clone's ledger. Fail-open."""
    try:
        with open(kf11_ledger(primary), "a") as f:
            f.write(json.dumps({"day": dt.date.today().isoformat(), "agent": agent, "branch": branch,
                                "at": dt.datetime.now().astimezone().isoformat(timespec="seconds"), "files": files}) + "\n")
    except Exception:
        pass


def kf11_summary(top, pin, content, session_n, day_n, agent, verdict=None):
    """The per-file diff summary a reviewer reads, with the line that keeps the answer: the section land writes
    into the handoff."""
    rows, status = {}, {}
    for line in run(["git", "diff", "--numstat", "-z", "--no-renames", pin, "HEAD"], top, check=False).stdout.split("\0"):
        bits = line.split("\t")
        if len(bits) == 3:
            rows[bits[2]] = (bits[0], bits[1])
    raw = run(["git", "diff", "--name-status", "-z", "--no-renames", pin, "HEAD"], top, check=False).stdout.split("\0")
    for i in range(0, len(raw) - 1, 2):
        status[raw[i + 1]] = raw[i]
    head = git(top, "rev-parse", "--short=10", "HEAD", check=False)
    lines = [KF11_START, "## KF-11 review: a content change over the threshold", "",
             f"Rule KF-11 (`{KF11_RULE}`, under the Workspaces root): any content change over {KF11_THRESHOLD} files, "
             "counted per session and per day, lands only as a reviewed diff.", "",
             f"- This land: {len(content)} content file(s). This session in all: {session_n}. "
             f"Today, by agent `{agent}` in this repository: {day_n}. Threshold: {KF11_THRESHOLD}.",
             f"- Base `{pin[:10]}`, head `{head}` (the head before the squash). The whole diff: `git diff {pin[:10]} {head}`.",
             "- Reviewer: read the table and the diff, then say yes or no. The answer is recorded here by "
             "`python3 .agents/bin/session land --kf11-verdict yes --kf11-reviewer NAME` (or `no`, which stops the land).",
             f"- Reviewer verdict: {_kf11_verdict_text(verdict)}", "",
             "| File | Change | Added | Removed |", "|---|---|---|---|"]
    for p in content[:KF11_ROWS]:
        add, rem = rows.get(p, ("?", "?"))
        lines.append(f"| `{p}` | {status.get(p, '?')} | {add} | {rem} |")
    if len(content) > KF11_ROWS:
        lines.append(f"| and {len(content) - KF11_ROWS} more | | | |")
    lines.append(KF11_END)
    return "\n".join(lines) + "\n"


def kf11_given_verdict(a):
    """The reviewer's answer passed to this land (kit 34), or None. Checked before land changes anything."""
    if not getattr(a, "kf11_verdict", None):
        if getattr(a, "kf11_reviewer", None):
            sys.exit("session: --kf11-reviewer goes with --kf11-verdict yes|no; nothing was changed")
        return None
    who = " ".join((getattr(a, "kf11_reviewer", None) or "").split())[:80]
    if not who:
        sys.exit("session: --kf11-verdict needs --kf11-reviewer NAME (the person who read the diff); nothing was changed")
    return {"verdict": a.kf11_verdict, "reviewer": who,
            "at": dt.datetime.now().astimezone().isoformat(timespec="seconds")}


def kf11_check(a, top, primary, pin, task, branch, verdict=None):
    """Measure this land against KF-11. Over the threshold: print the advisory, write the review section (with the
    reviewer's verdict, or the blank line that waits for it) into the handoff and commit it (a .agents/ path, so it
    adds nothing to the count). A verdict of no refuses the land; under enforcement every route but --no-merge is
    refused unless the verdict is yes, which lands through a PR. Returns (agent, content files of this land,
    the reason the land must take the PR route, or "")."""
    content, narrowed = kf11_classify(top, name_list(top, "--no-renames", pin, "HEAD"))
    agent = kf11_agent(task, branch)
    day_start = dt.datetime.now().astimezone().replace(hour=0, minute=0, second=0, microsecond=0)
    mine, today = kf11_earlier(top, primary, pin, branch, agent, day_start)
    session_n, day_n = len(mine | set(content)), len(today | set(content))
    EVENT["attrs"].update(kf11_files=len(content), kf11_session_files=session_n, kf11_day_files=day_n,
                          kf11_threshold=KF11_THRESHOLD, kf11_not_prose_files=narrowed)
    if session_n <= KF11_THRESHOLD and day_n <= KF11_THRESHOLD:
        EVENT["attrs"]["kf11"] = "under"
        if verdict and verdict["verdict"] == "no":
            EVENT["attrs"].update(kf11="rejected", kf11_verdict="no", reason="kf11")
            sys.exit("session: refused: the reviewer's verdict is no; nothing was pushed (this land is under the KF-11 "
                     "threshold, so there is no review block to record it in)")
        if verdict:
            print("KF-11: this land is under the threshold, so there is no review block; the verdict was not recorded.")
        return agent, content, ""
    pol = policy(top)
    enforce = (os.environ.get("SESSION_KF11_ENFORCE", "").strip() == "1"
               or (isinstance(pol.get("kf11"), dict) and pol["kf11"].get("enforce") is True))
    over = [x for x, n in (("this session", session_n), (f"today's lands by {agent}", day_n)) if n > KF11_THRESHOLD]
    rel = task.get("handoff") or f".agents/handoffs/{branch.replace('/', '-')}.md"
    recorded = None
    try:
        h = top / rel
        old = h.read_text() if h.is_file() else f"# Agent handoff: {branch}\n"
        cut = re.compile(re.escape(KF11_START) + r".*?" + re.escape(KF11_END) + r"\n?", re.S)
        h.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        h.write_text(cut.sub("", old).rstrip("\n") + "\n\n"
                     + kf11_summary(top, pin, content, session_n, day_n, agent, verdict))
        git(top, "add", "--", rel)
        if git(top, "status", "--porcelain", "--untracked-files=no", env=READ_ONLY):
            msg = "session: KF-11 review summary" + (f" (verdict {verdict['verdict']})" if verdict else "")
            if pushed(top):
                commit(top, task.get("provider", "claude"), "-q", "-m", msg)
            else:
                commit(top, task.get("provider", "claude"), "-q", "--amend", "--no-edit")
        recorded = kf11_read_verdict(h.read_text())  # read back: the line on disk, not the flag, is the record
        where = f"The per-file review summary is in {rel}"
    except SystemExit:
        raise
    except Exception as e:  # the advisory never fails a land it could not document
        where = f"The review summary could not be written ({type(e).__name__})"
    said = (recorded or {}).get("verdict") or (verdict or {}).get("verdict")
    EVENT["attrs"]["kf11_verdict"] = said or "blank"
    print(f"\nKF-11 ADVISORY: content changes over {KF11_THRESHOLD} files ({', '.join(over)}): this land {len(content)}, "
          f"this session {session_n}, today by {agent} {day_n}.\n"
          f"KF-11 says a change this size lands only as a reviewed diff (rule: {KF11_RULE}, under the Workspaces root).\n"
          f"{where}. To land it for review: `python3 .agents/bin/session land --no-merge` opens a PR and leaves it "
          "for a human; their answer is recorded with `--kf11-verdict yes|no --kf11-reviewer NAME`.")
    if said == "no":
        EVENT["attrs"].update(kf11="rejected", reason="kf11")
        sys.exit(f"session: refused: the reviewer's verdict is no ({_kf11_verdict_text(verdict)}), recorded in {rel} "
                 "and committed on this branch; nothing was pushed")
    if said == "yes":
        # the PR the review was read on (a branch pushed by an earlier --no-merge land) is the one that merges
        on_origin = bool(git(top, "rev-parse", "--verify", "-q", f"refs/remotes/origin/{branch}", check=False))
        why = ("KF-11 enforcement: a reviewed change lands as a pull request" if enforce else
               "KF-11: the reviewed pull request merges with the verdict" if on_origin else "")
        print(f"KF-11 reviewer verdict: {_kf11_verdict_text(verdict)}" + (f". {why}." if why else "") + "\n")
        EVENT["attrs"]["kf11"] = "approved"
        return agent, content, why
    if enforce and not a.no_merge:
        EVENT["attrs"].update(kf11="refused", reason="kf11")
        sys.exit("session: refused under KF-11 enforcement (SESSION_KF11_ENFORCE=1, or kf11.enforce in "
                 ".agents/repository-policy.json); nothing was pushed. Run `python3 .agents/bin/session land --no-merge` "
                 "so a human reviews the diff, then land with `--kf11-verdict yes --kf11-reviewer NAME`")
    EVENT["attrs"]["kf11"] = "reviewed_pr" if a.no_merge else "advisory"
    if not a.no_merge:
        print("KF-11 is advisory today, so this land goes on. SESSION_KF11_ENFORCE=1 turns it into a refusal.\n")
    return agent, content, ""


# The signal stub (kit 33). On 2026-10-31 a sweep found five products busy with commits and silent on signals;
# reading them showed the common cause: the session routine writes a handoff and a task file and never a signal,
# so a decision reaches the record only when someone chooses to write one, and the sweeps that look for dark zones
# read signals, never handoffs (SIG-DARK-ZONES-FIVE-PRODUCTS-ROOT-CAUSE-2026-10-31 at the Workspaces root,
# control 1). Now `land` puts the path at the source: when the handoff's "Decisions made" or "Unresolved risks or
# decisions" field is filled and the change itself adds or changes no signal file, land writes
# .intent/signals/SIG-<repo>-<task>-<date>.md with the handoff's own lines copied verbatim and the commit range,
# creates the folder and a CONTEXT.md when they are absent, and lands it with the work. It never writes content of
# its own beyond that frame, the property the engagement touch emitter already had. Skipped, and said why to
# Witness: a robot session (`start --robot`, or a task name under the policy's robot_tasks), no handoff, fields that
# are empty or say none, a change that already touches a signal, and the same words already in a stub this kit
# wrote for the task. Never a prompt: sessions are often unattended. Fail-open: an error here leaves the index as it
# was, prints one note, reports signal_stub=error to Witness, and the land goes on.
SIGNALS_DIR = ".intent/signals"
STUB_FIELDS = ("Decisions made", "Unresolved risks or decisions")
STUB_EMPTY = {"", "none", "n/a", "na", "no", "nil", "nothing", "tbd", "todo", "none yet", "not yet", "pending",
              "no decisions", "no risks", "nothing new", "nothing yet", "no new decisions", "no unresolved risks"}
STUB_MARK = "derived_by: session land"
SIGNAL_FILE = re.compile(r"(^|/)\.intent/signals/[^/]+\.md$")
FIELD_LINE = re.compile(r"^- ([^:`][^:]*?):(?:\s+(.*))?\s*$")
SIGNALS_CONTEXT = """---
title: Signals for {repo}
type: context
maturity: active
created: {day}
---

# Signals (.intent/signals/)

One file per thing noticed in this repository: a decision worth keeping, a surprise, a risk, a gap between what
was meant and what is. The folder is the record a later session, a sweep or a person reads cold; a decision that
lives only in a handoff, a commit message or a chat is invisible to them.

- Add one when a session decides something that a stranger could not re-derive from the code, when a risk is left
  open on purpose, or when something broke in a way worth remembering.
- Name: `SIG-<SUBJECT-IN-CAPS>-<YYYY-MM-DD>.md`. Frontmatter: `id`, `title`, `type: signal`, `status`, `severity`,
  `created`. Body in plain words: what was noticed with one concrete instance, why it matters, what would change.
- Status: `open` until someone acts; `resolved` only with `upstream_control_path` (the file that prevents a
  recurrence) and `catch_mechanism` (the check that flags one); otherwise `symptom-repaired, upstream-pending`
  (WS-DDR-113). A signal is never deleted; a superseded one says so and points at its successor.
- `session land` writes `SIG-{repo}-<task>-<date>.md` here when a session's handoff has "Decisions made" or
  "Unresolved risks or decisions" filled and the change carries no signal of its own (kit 33). Those stubs quote
  the handoff verbatim and carry `derived_by: session land`; triage them like any other signal.

This file was written by `session land` when it created the folder; the repository owns it from then on.
"""


def handoff_fields(text):
    """{field: [lines]} for STUB_FIELDS: the value on the field's own `- Field: ...` line, then every following
    line until the next top-level `- Field:` line, a heading or an HTML marker (the KF-11 review block). Indented
    sub-bullets and plain continuation lines are both kept; blank lines inside are kept, outer ones dropped."""
    out, cur = {}, None
    for line in text.splitlines():
        m = FIELD_LINE.match(line)
        if m and not line.startswith("  "):
            cur = m.group(1).strip() if m.group(1).strip() in STUB_FIELDS else None
            if cur:
                out[cur] = [m.group(2).rstrip()] if m.group(2) and m.group(2).strip() else []
            continue
        if line.lstrip().startswith("<!--"):
            break  # the KF-11 review block (kit 23) and anything after it: a table, never the fields
        if cur is None:
            continue
        if line.startswith("#"):
            cur = None
            continue
        out[cur].append(line.rstrip())
    for k in list(out):
        v = out[k]
        while v and not v[0].strip():
            v.pop(0)
        while v and not v[-1].strip():
            v.pop()
    return out


def stub_empty(lines):
    """True when the field says nothing: blank, or a placeholder such as none, n/a, tbd (punctuation ignored)."""
    norm = " ".join(re.sub(r"[.,;:`*_()\[\]/-]+", "", " ".join(lines)).split()).lower()
    return norm in STUB_EMPTY


def robot_task(task, branch, pol):
    """A scheduled job's session: `start --robot` wrote robot into the record, or the task name (the branch's last
    segment without the date the kit adds) starts with a prefix listed under robot_tasks in the policy (kit 29)."""
    if task.get("robot") is True:
        return True
    name = re.sub(r"^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}-", "", (branch or "").rsplit("/", 1)[-1])
    return any(isinstance(p, str) and p and name.startswith(p) for p in pol.get("robot_tasks", []) or [])


def stub_text(primary, task, branch, base, pin, head, rel, fields, day, sid):
    quoted = {k: "\n".join(v) if v else "(the handoff left this field empty)" for k, v in fields.items()}
    task_name = re.sub(r"^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}-", "", branch.rsplit("/", 1)[-1])
    q = lambda s: '"' + str(s).replace("\\", "\\\\").replace('"', '\\"') + '"'
    fm = [("id", sid), ("title", q(f"{primary.name}: {task_name} landed with decisions or risks in its handoff")),
          ("type", "signal"), ("status", "open"), ("severity", "low"), ("created", day),
          ("source_handoff", rel), ("derived_by", "session land"), ("kit_version", KIT_VERSION),
          ("repo", primary.name), ("task", task.get("id", "")), ("branch", branch), ("commit_range", f"{pin[:10]}..{head[:10]}")]
    lines = ["---"] + [f"{k}: {v}" for k, v in fm if v != ""] + ["---", "",
             f"# {primary.name}: {task_name} landed with decisions or risks in its handoff", "",
             "`session land` derived this stub from the session's handoff: the lines below are the handoff's own words, "
             "copied verbatim, because a decision that lives only in a handoff is invisible to the sweeps that read "
             "signals (SIG-DARK-ZONES-FIVE-PRODUCTS-ROOT-CAUSE-2026-10-31 at the Workspaces root, control 1). The kit "
             "wrote nothing beyond this frame. A person or a later session triages it: keep it open as the decision's "
             "record, raise its severity, or resolve it with `upstream_control_path` and `catch_mechanism`.", ""]
    for k in STUB_FIELDS:
        lines += [f"## {k} (from the handoff)", "", quoted[k], ""]
    lines += ["## Commit range", "",
              f"- Branch `{branch}`, commits `{pin[:10]}..{head[:10]}` on top of origin/{base} `{pin[:10]}`; "
              f"`session land` squashes them into one commit on {base}.",
              f"- Handoff: `{rel}`.", ""]
    return "\n".join(lines)


def task_for_branch(top, branch):
    """The task record naming this branch among every record in the worktree: a second land from one worktree
    has no record in origin/<base>...HEAD any more (the first land carried it), yet its handoff still speaks."""
    for p in task_files(top):
        try:
            rec = json.loads(p.read_text())
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            continue
        if isinstance(rec, dict) and rec.get("branch") == branch:
            return rec
    return {}


def _signal_stub(top, primary, pin, task, branch, base):
    # Kit 39 (2026-11-16, process review rank 7, Brien: "Yes, all five"): the per-land stub is off by default. The
    # decision lives in the commit and the Witness event; a repo that wants the old stub sets "signal_stub": true.
    if policy(top).get("signal_stub") is not True:
        EVENT["attrs"]["signal_stub"] = "skipped_off"
        return
    task = task or task_for_branch(top, branch)
    if not task:
        EVENT["attrs"]["signal_stub"] = "skipped_no_task"
        return
    if robot_task(task, branch, policy(top)):
        EVENT["attrs"]["signal_stub"] = "skipped_robot"
        return
    rel = task.get("handoff") or ""
    h = top / rel if rel else None
    if not (h and h.is_file()):
        EVENT["attrs"]["signal_stub"] = "skipped_no_handoff"
        return
    fields = handoff_fields(h.read_text(errors="replace"))
    filled = {k: v for k, v in fields.items() if not stub_empty(v)}
    if not filled:
        EVENT["attrs"]["signal_stub"] = "skipped_empty"
        return
    if any(SIGNAL_FILE.search(p) for p in name_list(top, "--no-renames", pin, "HEAD")):
        EVENT["attrs"]["signal_stub"] = "skipped_has_signal"
        return
    sig_dir = top / SIGNALS_DIR
    day = dt.date.today().isoformat()
    task_name = re.sub(r"^\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}-", "", branch.rsplit("/", 1)[-1]) or task.get("id", "task")
    stem = f"SIG-{primary.name}-{task_name}-{day}"
    verbatim = [l for v in filled.values() for l in v if l.strip()]
    for earlier in sorted(sig_dir.glob(f"SIG-{primary.name}-{task_name}-*.md")) if sig_dir.is_dir() else []:
        t = earlier.read_text(errors="replace")
        if STUB_MARK in t and all(l in t for l in verbatim):
            EVENT["attrs"]["signal_stub"] = "skipped_already_recorded"
            return
    path, n = sig_dir / f"{stem}.md", 2
    while path.exists():
        path, n = sig_dir / f"{stem}-{n}.md", n + 1
    head = git(top, "rev-parse", "HEAD")
    fields = {k: filled.get(k, []) for k in STUB_FIELDS}
    text = stub_text(primary, task, branch, base, pin, head, rel, fields, day, path.stem)
    sig_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    wrote = [path]
    ctx = sig_dir / "CONTEXT.md"
    if not ctx.exists():
        ctx.write_text(SIGNALS_CONTEXT.format(repo=primary.name, day=day))
        wrote.append(ctx)
    path.write_text(text)
    git(top, "add", "--", *[str(p.relative_to(top)) for p in wrote])
    if pushed(top):
        commit(top, task.get("provider", "claude"), "-q", "-m", f"session: signal stub {path.stem}")
    else:
        commit(top, task.get("provider", "claude"), "-q", "--amend", "--no-edit")
    EVENT["attrs"].update(signal_stub="written", signal_stub_fields=len(filled))
    print(f"signal stub: {path.relative_to(top)} (the handoff's {' and '.join(filled)}, quoted; triage it like any signal)")


def signal_stub_check(top, primary, pin, task, branch, base):
    """Kit 33, fail-open: whatever goes wrong here, the index is left as it was, one note is printed and the land
    goes on; Witness gets signal_stub=error with the exception's name."""
    try:
        _signal_stub(top, primary, pin, task, branch, base)
    except (Exception, SystemExit) as e:
        run(["git", "reset", "-q", "--", SIGNALS_DIR], top, check=False)
        EVENT["attrs"].update(signal_stub="error", signal_stub_error=type(e).__name__)
        print(f"note: the signal stub from the handoff could not be written ({type(e).__name__}); the land goes on "
              f"(reported to Witness)")


def land_gates(a, top, primary, pin, task_rec, branch, base, verdict, retry=False):
    """Every check a land runs on the tree it would push: the signal stub, KF-11, the repository audit, the
    repository's own pre-land commands and the portability check. Returns kf11_check's (agent, content, pr reason).

    Kit 37 (V1, 31-session-kit-implicit-states.md U5): until kit 36 the retry after a push race (a sibling landed
    between this land's checks and its push) reran only the pre-land commands and the portability check, so a tree
    the audit refuses (a register with a key used twice, an ownership overlap) could reach main. The retry now calls
    this same function. The first run's signal stub result is kept on a retry: the stub it wrote is in the change,
    so the second run reports skipped_has_signal for the same stub."""
    if retry:
        EVENT["attrs"]["land_gate_runs"] = EVENT["attrs"].get("land_gate_runs", 1) + 1
        first_stub = EVENT["attrs"].get("signal_stub")
    else:
        EVENT["attrs"]["land_gate_runs"] = 1
    # The signal stub (kit 33) comes first, so KF-11 counts it and the gates judge it with the rest
    signal_stub_check(top, primary, pin, task_rec, branch, base)
    if retry and first_stub and EVENT["attrs"].get("signal_stub") == "skipped_has_signal":
        EVENT["attrs"]["signal_stub"] = first_stub
    # KF-11 (kit 23): measured on the rebased tree, before the gates, so its handoff commit is judged with the rest
    kf = kf11_check(a, top, primary, pin, task_rec, branch, verdict)
    gate = pre_land_commands(top)  # read first: a broken declaration stops the land with its own reason
    audit = top / ".agents" / "bin" / "agent_repo_audit.py"
    if audit.exists():
        ar = run([sys.executable, str(audit), "--check", "--against", pin], top, check=False)
        print(ar.stdout.strip())
        # kit 36: the date-folder warnings (WS-DDR-152) ride on this land's one Witness event, as a count only
        EVENT["attrs"]["date_folder_warnings"] = sum(1 for l in ar.stdout.splitlines() if l.startswith("WARNING: date-named folder"))
        if ar.returncode:
            if retry:
                EVENT["attrs"]["reason"] = "audit_after_rebase"
                sys.exit("session: audit found errors after the rebase onto another session's landing; nothing was "
                         "pushed; fix them and land again")
            sys.exit("session: audit found errors; fix them and land again")
    run_pre_land(top, gate)  # the tree that would land: this branch rebased onto origin/<base> (kit 18)
    if not portability_check(top):
        sys.exit("session: portability check failed" + (" after the rebase" if retry else
                 " (the check CI used to run on every push)") + "; fix it and land again")
    return kf


def land_refused(top, t, branch):
    """Kit 40 (M27): after a land refused on a conflict with someone else's landing, the task's record must not read
    complete, and the refusal must be where both owners can see it. The close this land made is reopened (status
    blocked, the landing it conflicts with by commit and author, the files), folded into the same unpushed commit or
    added as one more, and the branch is backed up now (a land turns the commit hook's backup off for itself), so the
    refused work and its record are on the remote, where the other owner's `status --all` shows the refusal."""
    EVENT["attrs"].update(reason="owners_decide", conflict_landings=len(CONFLICT.get("landings") or []))
    try:
        if t and t.is_file():
            task = json.loads(t.read_text())
            task["status"] = "blocked"
            task.pop("landed", None)
            task["land_refused"] = {
                "at": dt.datetime.now().astimezone().isoformat(timespec="seconds"),
                "files": (CONFLICT.get("files") or [])[:50],
                "landings": [{k: r[k] for k in ("commit", "author", "email", "date", "branch")}
                             for r in (CONFLICT.get("landings") or [])[:10]],
                "decision": "open: the two owners decide; no agent chooses"}
            t.write_text(json.dumps(task, indent=2) + "\n")
            git(top, "add", str(t.relative_to(top)))
            h = top / task.get("handoff", "")
            if h.is_file():
                h.write_text(re.sub(r"^- Status: .*$", "- Status: blocked", h.read_text(), count=1, flags=re.M))
                git(top, "add", str(h.relative_to(top)))
            if pushed(top):
                commit(top, task.get("provider", "claude"), "-q", "-m",
                       f"session: land refused for {task['id']}, the owners decide")
            else:
                commit(top, task.get("provider", "claude"), "-q", "--amend", "--no-edit")
            print(f"The task record {t.relative_to(top)} reads blocked, not complete, and names the landing.")
        if branch.startswith(WIP_BRANCHES):
            run([sys.executable, str(Path(__file__).resolve()), "wip-backup", "--quiet"], top, check=False,
                env={WIP_SWITCH: "1"})
            print(f"The refused work is backed up as {wip_ref(branch)}: `python3 .agents/bin/session status --all` in "
                  "the other owner's clone shows it and this refusal.")
    except (Exception, SystemExit) as e:  # noqa: BLE001 - the refusal stands whether or not its record could be written
        print(f"note: the refusal could not be written into the task record ({type(e).__name__}); the land is still refused")


def cmd_land(a):
    top, primary, linked = here(a.here)
    if not linked:
        sys.exit("session: land runs inside a session worktree, not the primary checkout")
    verdict = kf11_given_verdict(a)  # a malformed verdict refuses before anything changes (kit 34)
    os.environ[WIP_SWITCH] = "0"  # land's own commits (the close, a rebase's replays) push no backup ref
    base = default_branch(primary)
    if git(top, "status", "--porcelain", "--untracked-files=no", env=READ_ONLY):
        sys.exit("session: commit or set aside your tracked changes first (never stash another session's work)")
    if os.path.exists(os.path.join(git(top, "rev-parse", "--git-dir"), "rebase-merge")):
        sys.exit("session: a rebase is in progress; resolve by meaning, `git add`, `git rebase --continue`, then land again")
    put_back(top)  # what a land stopped by a conflict kept aside, now that its rebase has finished
    git(top, "fetch", "-q", "origin", base)
    activate_prepush(top)
    t = my_task(top, f"origin/{base}")
    if t:
        task = json.loads(t.read_text())
        decided = (getattr(a, "owners_decided", None) or "").strip()
        if task.get("status") != "complete" or (decided and (task.get("conflict_decision") or {}).get("text") != decided):
            task["status"] = "complete"
            task["landed"] = dt.datetime.now().astimezone().isoformat(timespec="seconds")
            if decided:  # kit 40: the owners' decision after a refused land, on the record that lands
                task["conflict_decision"] = {"text": decided, "at": task["landed"],
                                             "recorded_by": git(top, "var", "GIT_AUTHOR_IDENT", check=False).rsplit(">", 1)[0] + ">"}
                if isinstance(task.get("land_refused"), dict):
                    task["land_refused"]["decision"] = "decided: see conflict_decision"
            t.write_text(json.dumps(task, indent=2) + "\n")
            h = top / task.get("handoff", "")
            if h.is_file():
                h.write_text(re.sub(r"^- Status: .*$", "- Status: complete", h.read_text(), count=1, flags=re.M))
                git(top, "add", str(h.relative_to(top)))
            git(top, "add", str(t.relative_to(top)))
            # Fold the close into the session's own last commit when that commit has not left the
            # machine: a separate `session: close` commit cost its own CI cycle (about 20 percent of
            # session CI, measured 2026-09-24). A commit already pushed is never amended.
            ahead = git(top, "rev-list", "--count", f"origin/{base}..HEAD") != "0"
            if ahead and not pushed(top):
                commit(top, task.get("provider", "claude"), "-q", "--amend", "--no-edit")
            else:
                commit(top, task.get("provider", "claude"), "-q", "-m", f"session: close {task['id']}")
    branch = git(top, "branch", "--show-current")
    if git(top, "rev-list", "--count", f"origin/{base}..HEAD") == "0":
        sys.exit("session: nothing to land")
    decided = (getattr(a, "owners_decided", None) or "").strip()
    pin = rebase_on_base(top, base, branch, decided)
    if not pin:
        if CONFLICT.get("refused"):
            land_refused(top, t, branch)
        return 3
    if git(top, "rev-list", "--count", f"{pin}..HEAD") == "0":
        print(f"nothing new to land: everything on this branch is already on origin/{base}")
        return 0
    task_rec = json.loads(t.read_text()) if t and t.is_file() else {}
    kf_agent, kf_content, kf_pr = land_gates(a, top, primary, pin, task_rec, branch, base, verdict)
    changed = name_list(top, "--no-renames", f"{pin}...HEAD")
    EVENT["attrs"]["files"] = len(changed)
    # Local landing is the default (WS-DDR-143 amendment 2026-09-24): the checks already ran here,
    # so a PR would only buy a second, billed run of them. A PR is used for ask paths (a human
    # decides), for a base branch that requires one, or when asked for with --pr or --no-merge.
    hits = ask_hits(changed, policy(top))
    why_pr = ("--pr" if a.pr else "--no-merge" if a.no_merge else
              kf_pr if kf_pr else
              f"touches ask paths {sorted(set(hits))[:8]}" if hits and not a.local else
              "" if a.local else requires_pr(top, base))
    if not why_pr:
        for attempt in range(3):
            head = git(top, "rev-parse", "HEAD")
            # the parent is the commit this branch was rebased onto (pin), never the shared origin/<base>
            # ref re-read here: a sibling worktree's push moves that ref during the gate (kit 19)
            squash = git(top, "commit-tree", f"{head}^{{tree}}", "-p", pin, "-m", land_message(top, base, a.title, branch, since=pin))
            foreign = foreign_paths(top, squash, pin, pin, head)
            if foreign:
                EVENT["attrs"]["reason"] = "foreign_paths"
                sys.exit(f"session: refused: the squash would change {len(foreign)} path(s) this branch never touched "
                         f"({names(foreign)}); that is another session's landing being reverted. Nothing was pushed. "
                         "Run `python3 .agents/bin/session land` again, and report this to the kit's owner")
            p = run(["git", "push", "-q", "origin", f"{squash}:refs/heads/{base}"], top, check=False)
            if p.returncode == 0:
                EVENT["attrs"]["route"] = "local"
                print(f"landed locally as {squash[:8]} on origin/{base} (no PR, no CI run)")
                kf11_record(primary, kf_agent, branch, kf_content)
                drop_wip(top, branch)  # landed: the backup ref has done its job (kit 29)
                # the branch follows its squash (same tree): a later land replays only what is new
                follow(top, squash, head, base)
                ff_primary(primary, base)
                print("Next: python3 .agents/bin/session finish")
                return 0
            err = (p.stderr or p.stdout).strip()
            if re.search(r"protected branch|GH006|GH013|rule violations|required status check|pull request", err, re.I):
                why_pr = f"the push to {base} was refused: {' '.join(err.split())[:160]}"
                break
            if "fetch first" not in err and "non-fast-forward" not in err and "rejected" not in err:
                sys.exit(f"session: push to {base} failed:\n{err}")
            # someone landed in between: take their change and go again
            git(top, "fetch", "-q", "origin", base)
            pin = rebase_on_base(top, base, branch, decided)
            if not pin:
                if CONFLICT.get("refused"):
                    land_refused(top, t, branch)
                return 3
            # a new tree: the other session's landing is in it, so every gate judges it again (kit 37, V1)
            kf_agent, kf_content, kf_pr = land_gates(a, top, primary, pin, task_rec, branch, base, verdict, retry=True)
            if kf_pr:
                why_pr = kf_pr
                break
        else:
            sys.exit(f"session: origin/{base} kept moving; run `python3 .agents/bin/session land` again")
    EVENT["attrs"]["route"] = "pr"
    print(f"Landing through a PR: {why_pr}")
    # prune first: a branch GitHub deleted on an earlier merge leaves a stale origin/<branch> that
    # makes --force-with-lease refuse the push
    run(["git", "fetch", "-q", "--prune", "origin"], top, check=False)
    head = git(top, "rev-parse", "HEAD")
    git(top, "push", "-q", "--force-with-lease", "-u", "origin", branch)
    # an open PR only: `gh pr view <branch>` also returns the branch's merged PR from an earlier land
    pr = run(["gh", "pr", "list", *gh_repo(top), "--head", branch, "--base", base, "--state", "open",
              "--json", "url", "-q", ".[0].url // empty"], top, check=False)
    if pr.returncode or not pr.stdout.strip():
        subjects = git(top, "log", "--reverse", "--format=%s", f"origin/{base}..HEAD").splitlines()
        title = a.title or next((x for x in subjects if not x.startswith("session: close")), branch)
        body = "Session branch landed under .agents/SESSIONS.md.\n\nChanged: " + ", ".join(f"`{c}`" for c in changed[:30])
        if t:
            h = top / json.loads(t.read_text()).get("handoff", "")
            if h.is_file():
                text = h.read_text()
                i = text.find(KF11_START)  # a KF-11 review section always reaches the PR description whole
                body = text[:6000] if i < 0 else (text[:min(i, 3000)].rstrip() + "\n\n" + text[i:])[:60000]
        pr = run(["gh", "pr", "create", *gh_repo(top), "--head", branch, "--base", base, "--title", title, "--body", body + FOOTER], top)
    url = pr.stdout.strip().splitlines()[-1]
    print(f"PR: {url}")
    kf11_record(primary, kf_agent, branch, kf_content)
    if hits or a.no_merge:
        print("Left open for a human" + (f": touches ask paths {sorted(set(hits))[:8]}" if hits else ""))
        return 0
    m = run(["gh", "pr", "merge", url, *gh_repo(top), "--squash", "--delete-branch", "--auto"], top, check=False)
    if m.returncode:
        m = run(["gh", "pr", "merge", url, *gh_repo(top), "--squash", "--delete-branch"], top, check=False)
    info = run(["gh", "pr", "view", url, *gh_repo(top), "--json", "state,autoMergeRequest,mergeCommit", "-q",
                "[.state, (.autoMergeRequest != null), (.mergeCommit.oid // \"\")] | @tsv"], top, check=False).stdout.strip().split("\t")
    state, armed, merged = (info + ["", "", ""])[:3]
    if state == "MERGED":
        print("PR merged.")
        drop_wip(top, branch)
        ff_primary(primary, base)
        if not (merged and follow(top, merged, head, base)):
            print("branch still carries the merged commits (the squash is not this head's tree); "
                  "the next land from this worktree skips them")
        print("Next: python3 .agents/bin/session finish")
    elif armed == "true":
        print("Auto-merge armed: the PR squash-merges by itself when its checks pass.\n"
              f"Until then nothing moves the primary: it lags origin/{base} after the merge until\n"
              "`python3 .agents/bin/session finish` runs or the hub's primary-ff sweep passes (every 30 min).\n"
              "Then: python3 .agents/bin/session finish")
    else:
        print(f"PR state: {state or 'unknown'}, not merged" + (f"\n{m.stderr.strip()}" if m.returncode else ""))
    return 0


def pick_worktree(primary, linked, top, task, verb):
    """The worktree a finish or retire acts on: the one the shell is in, or from the primary the one --task names."""
    if linked:
        return top
    cands = [s for s in sessions(primary) if task and task in (s["branch"] + s["task"] + s["path"])]
    if len(cands) != 1:
        sys.exit(f"session: from the primary checkout, name the session with --task (it matched {len(cands)})")
    return Path(cands[0]["path"])


def keep_ignored(primary, wt):
    """Keep every ignored file before finish removes the worktree, with retire's own helpers (kit 35).

    `git worktree remove`, even without --force, deletes ignored files and exits 0: a local credentials file, a client
    original kept out of git for its size, a pulled dataset. `retire` and the reaper move them first; through kit 34
    `finish` did not (U8 in Core/products/_intake/2026-11-09-resilient-ways-of-working/31-session-kit-implicit-states.md,
    checked in a throwaway repository). Now, as in retire (retirement.ignored_plan, keep_aside, carry_ignored): a file
    the primary lacks is moved there; an identical copy is left to go; where the primary holds a different copy, the
    newer takes the primary's path and the older is kept in .agents/residue/<worktree>/ignored-copies-<date>.tar.gz
    (ignored through this clone's info/exclude); regenerable files (.venv, node_modules, caches) go with the worktree.
    More ignored bytes to compare than retirement.LARGE_IGNORED_BYTES, or a copy that cannot be confirmed, stops the
    finish with exit status 1 and nothing removed. The Witness event carries the counts, never a path."""
    rt = load_retirement()
    plan = rt.ignored_plan(primary, wt)
    counts = {"ignored_moved": 0, "ignored_replaced": 0, "ignored_aside": len(plan["aside"]),
              "ignored_same": len(plan["dup"]), "ignored_dropped": plan["dropped"]}
    EVENT["attrs"].update(counts)
    stop = (f"Nothing was removed: {wt} and its branch are as they were apart from files already moved to the primary. "
            "Move or delete the named files yourself, then run `session finish` again")
    if plan["work"]:
        EVENT["attrs"]["reason"] = "finish_ignored_refused"
        sys.exit(f"session: finish stopped before removing the worktree, because `git worktree remove` would delete "
                 f"ignored files the kit could not compare: {names(plan['work'])}.\n{stop}")
    aside = rt.aside_archive(primary, wt.name, dt.date.today().isoformat()) if plan["replace"] or plan["aside"] else None
    try:
        if aside:
            counts["ignored_replaced"] = rt.keep_aside(primary, wt, plan["replace"], plan["aside"], aside)
        counts["ignored_moved"] = rt.carry_ignored(primary, wt, plan["carry"])
    except (RuntimeError, OSError) as e:
        EVENT["attrs"].update(counts, reason="finish_ignored_refused")
        sys.exit(f"session: finish stopped before removing the worktree: an ignored file could not be kept ({e}).\n{stop}")
    EVENT["attrs"].update(counts)
    kept = counts["ignored_moved"] + counts["ignored_replaced"]
    if kept or aside:
        print(f"kept {kept} ignored file(s) in the primary checkout before removing the worktree"
              + (f"; {counts['ignored_replaced']} newer copy(ies) took the primary's place, and the copies they replaced "
                 f"plus {counts['ignored_aside']} older worktree copy(ies) are archived in {aside}" if aside else ""))
    return counts


def cmd_finish(a):
    top, primary, linked = here(a.here)
    os.environ[WIP_SWITCH] = "0"
    base = default_branch(primary)
    wt = pick_worktree(primary, linked, top, a.task, "finish")
    branch = git(wt, "branch", "--show-current")
    git(primary, "fetch", "-q", "--prune", "origin")
    st, pr_head = (run(["gh", "pr", "view", branch, *gh_repo(wt), "--json", "state,headRefOid", "-q",
                        "[.state, .headRefOid] | @tsv"], wt, check=False).stdout.strip().split("\t") + ["", ""])[:2]
    mine = name_list(wt, "--no-renames", f"origin/{base}...HEAD", check=False, env=READ_ONLY)
    contained = not mine or not name_list(wt, "HEAD", f"origin/{base}", "--", *mine, check=False, env=READ_ONLY)
    # a merged PR counts only when it merged this head: commits made after an earlier landing have not landed
    if not (st == "MERGED" and pr_head == git(wt, "rev-parse", "HEAD")) and not contained:
        if st == "MERGED":
            sys.exit(f"session: {branch} has commits after the head its merged PR carried; land them first")
        if st == "OPEN":
            sys.exit(f"session: the PR for {branch} is still open (checks running or awaiting a human); finish again once it merges")
        sys.exit(f"session: {branch} is not merged (PR state {st or 'none'}); land it first")
    left = put_back(wt)
    if left:
        sys.exit(f"session: land kept {names(left)} aside in {kept_dir(wt)} and could not put it back; removing the "
                 "worktree would delete that copy. Merge it by hand, delete the kept copy, then finish again")
    if git(wt, "status", "--porcelain", env=READ_ONLY):
        sys.exit(f"session: {wt} has uncommitted changes; they are not yours to discard")
    drop_wip(primary, branch)  # its work is on main: the backup ref goes (kit 29)
    ff_primary(primary, base)
    if "/.claude/worktrees/" in str(wt):
        print(f"left {wt} in place: the desktop app owns that worktree")
        return 0
    keep_ignored(primary, wt)  # `git worktree remove` deletes ignored files without asking (kit 35)
    os.chdir(primary)  # never remove the directory we are standing in
    git(primary, "worktree", "remove", str(wt))
    run(["git", "branch", "-D", branch], primary, check=False)
    print(f"removed {wt} and local branch {branch}")
    return 0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backups and retirement (kit 29).
#
# On 2026-10-23 twenty worktrees had sat 3 to 20 days with work that existed only on Aurelius's disk, one of
# them the only copy of a client chat extract: their sessions ended before `land` (killed, rate-limited, a
# person walked away, a scheduled job failed), a kit branch is never pushed before land, and the hourly reaper
# rightly refuses to remove unlanded work but said so only in a log nobody reads.
#   - Backup on every commit: .agents/hooks/post-commit starts `session wip-backup` in the background, which
#     force-pushes the branch tip to origin as refs/wip/<branch>. GitHub accepts a ref outside refs/heads and
#     refs/tags, lists it as no branch and runs no workflow for it (probed on theparlor/org-design-tooling,
#     whose workflow runs on every push, 2026-10-23). Only agent/* and claude/* branches; never the trunk.
#     Fail-open and quiet: a commit never waits on it or fails for it; offline it simply fails. git push over
#     ssh is all it needs (no gh), so it works on a machine where gh does not.
#   - `land`, `finish` and `retire` (and the reaper's retirements) delete the backup ref.
#   - `session retire`: retirement.retire() (beside this script) snapshots everything uncommitted into one
#     commit, tags it archive/<branch>-<date>, pushes the tag, lands a ledger row and manifest, and removes
#     the worktree. The reaper calls the same routine for a robot's superseded run.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WIP_SWITCH = "SESSION_WIP_BACKUP"  # "0" turns the backup push off for one command (land, finish, retire set it)
WIP_BRANCHES = ("agent/", "claude/")
WIP_TIMEOUT = 90  # seconds a backup push may take before it is given up


def wip_ref(branch):
    return f"refs/wip/{branch}"


def drop_wip(cwd, branch):
    """Delete the branch's backup ref on origin, and on the private backup remote when the policy names one (kit 37).
    Kit 39: only when its tip is this clone's work (retirement.guarded_drop): a ref holding another clone's commit
    under the same name is left, and the land or finish says whose it is. Fail-open: a missing ref or no network is
    fine."""
    if not branch or not branch.startswith(WIP_BRANCHES):
        return
    try:
        rt = load_retirement()
    except (Exception, SystemExit):
        return
    remotes = ["origin"]
    try:
        extra = rt.private_backup_remote(cwd)
        if extra and extra != "origin":
            remotes.append(extra)
    except (Exception, SystemExit):
        pass
    for remote in remotes:
        try:
            rt.drop_claim(cwd, branch, remote=remote, timeout=60, env=wip_env())  # kit 40: the claim goes with it
            gone, why = rt.guarded_drop(cwd, branch, remote=remote, timeout=60, env=wip_env())
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - fail-open
            continue
        if not gone and why.startswith("left "):
            EVENT["attrs"]["wip_left"] = EVENT["attrs"].get("wip_left", 0) + 1
            print(f"backup ref {why}; it is not this clone's to remove")


def wip_env():
    """No prompt, and an ssh that gives up quickly when the network is gone, unless ssh is configured already."""
    env = dict(os.environ, GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT="0", GIT_ASKPASS="true", SSH_ASKPASS="true")
    if not (env.get("GIT_SSH_COMMAND") or env.get("GIT_SSH")):
        env["GIT_SSH_COMMAND"] = "ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o ConnectTimeout=15"
    return env


def backup_destination(top):
    """(remote or None, origin visibility). Kit 37 (S0-07): unlanded commits go to origin only when origin is known to
    be private. A public origin, or one whose visibility cannot be told now (unknown counts as public here, so a
    network error never sends unlanded work to a possibly public repository), gets nothing: the push goes to the
    policy's private_backup_remote when one is named, else nowhere (None) and the work stays on this machine."""
    try:
        return load_retirement().backup_destination(top)  # kit 38: the one gate, shared with retire and the reaper
    except (Exception, SystemExit):
        return None, "unknown"


def cmd_wip_backup(a):
    """Push this branch's tip to origin as refs/wip/<branch>. Always exits 0: a backup never fails a commit."""
    top, primary, linked = here(a.here)
    branch = git(top, "branch", "--show-current", check=False, env=READ_ONLY)
    if os.environ.get(WIP_SWITCH, "").strip() == "0":
        EVENT["attrs"]["decision"] = "disabled"
        return 0
    if not branch.startswith(WIP_BRANCHES):
        EVENT["attrs"]["decision"] = "not_a_session_branch"
        return 0
    lock = Path(git(top, "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir", check=False)) / "wip-backup.lock"
    gitdir = git(top, "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-dir", check=False, env=READ_ONLY)
    remote, vis = backup_destination(top)
    EVENT["attrs"]["origin_visibility"] = vis
    if remote is None:
        EVENT["attrs"]["decision"] = "kept_local"
        tip = git(top, "rev-parse", "-q", "--verify", f"refs/heads/{branch}", check=False, env=READ_ONLY)
        record_backup(gitdir, "kept-local", tip, branch,
                      f"origin is {vis}; unlanded commits are not pushed there and no private_backup_remote is named; "
                      "on this machine only")
        if not a.quiet:
            print(f"session: backup of {branch} kept on this machine: origin is {vis} (kit 37)", file=sys.stderr)
        return 0
    import fcntl
    with open(lock, "a") as fh:
        deadline = time.monotonic() + WIP_TIMEOUT
        while True:  # one push at a time per clone; the one that waited pushes the newest tip
            try:
                fcntl.flock(fh, fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
                break
            except OSError:
                if time.monotonic() > deadline:
                    EVENT["attrs"]["decision"] = "lock_timeout"
                    record_backup(gitdir, "lock_timeout", git(top, "rev-parse", "-q", "--verify", f"refs/heads/{branch}",
                                                             check=False, env=READ_ONLY), branch,
                                  "another backup push held this clone's lock past the timeout")
                    return 0
                time.sleep(0.2)
        tip = git(top, "rev-parse", "-q", "--verify", f"refs/heads/{branch}", check=False, env=READ_ONLY)
        # Kit 39: never a blind forced push. The remote ref is read first and the push carries a lease on what it held;
        # a ref holding a commit this clone did not write (another person's clone that started the same task name) is
        # refused and left as it is, and this work stays on this machine (retirement.guarded_push).
        try:
            result, err = load_retirement().guarded_push(top, tip, wip_ref(branch), remote=remote, branch=branch,
                                                         gitdirs=(gitdir,), timeout=WIP_TIMEOUT, env=wip_env())
        except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 - a backup never fails a commit
            result, err = "error", f"{type(e).__name__}: {e}"
        rc = 0 if result in ("pushed", "current") else 1
    if rc == 0:
        EVENT["attrs"]["decision"] = "pushed" if remote == "origin" else "pushed-private"
    elif result == load_retirement().FOREIGN:
        EVENT["outcome"] = "blocked"
        EVENT["attrs"]["decision"] = "refused_foreign"
        if not a.quiet:
            print(f"session: backup of {branch} NOT pushed: {err}", file=sys.stderr)
        record_backup(gitdir, load_retirement().FOREIGN, tip, branch, err)
        return 0
    else:
        EVENT["outcome"] = "error"
        EVENT["attrs"]["decision"] = ("offline" if re.search(r"resolve host|timed out|Network is unreachable|"
                                                             r"Connection refused", err, re.I)
                                      else "archived" if re.search(r"archived|read-only", err, re.I) else "push_failed")
        if not a.quiet:
            print(f"session: backup push of {branch} failed: {' '.join(err.split())[:200]}", file=sys.stderr)
    record_backup(gitdir, EVENT["attrs"]["decision"], tip, branch, "" if rc == 0 else err)
    return 0


def record_backup(gitdir, result, tip, branch, detail=""):
    """Leave this push's result in the worktree's git dir (session-backup.json, kit 35), so `session status` and the
    reaper's stuck file can say what is not on GitHub and for how long. Fail-open: a record it cannot write changes
    nothing about the backup, and the Witness event says whether it was written."""
    try:
        rec = load_retirement().write_backup_record(gitdir, "commit", result, head=tip, target=tip,
                                                    ref=wip_ref(branch), detail=detail) if gitdir else None
    except (Exception, SystemExit):
        rec = None
    EVENT["attrs"]["recorded"] = rec is not None


_RETIREMENT = []


def load_retirement():
    """retirement.py beside this script (kit 29): the routine and record format shared with the worktree reaper.

    Loaded once per run and with no bytecode written (kit 35): since `status`, `finish` and the commit hook's backup
    load it too, a __pycache__ folder written beside it would sit untracked in every session worktree of a repository
    that does not ignore it, where `finish` reads it as uncommitted work and `status` as a changed file."""
    if _RETIREMENT:
        return _RETIREMENT[0]
    src = Path(__file__).resolve().parent / "retirement.py"
    if not src.is_file():
        sys.exit(f"session: {src} is missing; reinstall the session kit (kit 29 or later)")
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("session_kit_retirement", src)
    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    was, sys.dont_write_bytecode = sys.dont_write_bytecode, True
    try:
        spec.loader.exec_module(mod)
    finally:
        sys.dont_write_bytecode = was
    _RETIREMENT.append(mod)
    return mod


def cmd_retire(a):
    """Keep abandoned or superseded work on a pushed archive tag with a ledger row, then remove the worktree."""
    top, primary, linked = here(a.here)
    os.environ[WIP_SWITCH] = "0"
    wt = pick_worktree(primary, linked, top, a.task, "retire")
    rt = load_retirement()
    kind = "desktop" if "/.claude/worktrees/" in str(wt) else "kit"
    if not a.dry_run:
        os.chdir(primary)  # never remove the directory we are standing in
    rec = rt.retire(primary, wt, base=default_branch(primary), reason=a.reason or "retired by hand",
                    source="session-retire", kind=kind, dry_run=a.dry_run)
    EVENT["attrs"].update(action=rec["action"], snapshot_files=len(rec.get("snapshot") or []),
                          archived=bool(rec.get("archived")), route=rec.get("route", ""),
                          origin_visibility=rec.get("origin_visibility", ""), tag_remote=rec.get("tag_remote", ""),
                          freed_mb=rec.get("freed_mb") or 0, dry_run=bool(a.dry_run))
    if rec["action"] == "would-retire":
        print(f"would retire {wt}: {rec['reason']}; nothing changed (dry run)")
        return 0
    if rec["action"] != "retired":
        EVENT["attrs"]["reason"] = "retire_" + rec["action"]
        EVENT["outcome"] = "blocked" if rec["action"] == "refused" else "error"
        print(f"session: retire {rec['action']}: {rec['reason']}\nNothing was removed; {wt} is as it was"
              + (" apart from what the message names" if rec["action"] == "error" else ""), file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    where = ("tag kept LOCAL (origin is archived); ledger row in the Workspaces root" if rec.get("archived")
             else (f"origin is {rec.get('origin_visibility')}, so the tag is "
                   + ("on the private backup remote" if rec.get("remote_durable") else
                      "kept LOCAL, on this machine only (not remotely durable)")
                   + "; ledger row in the Workspaces root") if rec.get("withheld")
             else f"tag pushed; ledger row {'landed' if rec.get('route') == 'pushed' else 'in PR ' + str(rec.get('record'))}")
    print(f"retired {wt}\n  tag: {rec['tag']} ({where})\n  snapshot: {len(rec['snapshot'])} uncommitted path(s) "
          f"folded in\n  recover: git checkout -b {rec.get('branch') or '<branch>'} {rec['tag']}")
    return 0


def main():
    p = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="session", description="Parallel sessions: see .agents/SESSIONS.md")
    sub = p.add_subparsers(dest="cmd", required=True)
    other = "act on the repository the shell is in even though this script belongs to another one (kit 17)"
    s = sub.add_parser("start"); s.add_argument("task"); s.add_argument("--own", action="append", default=[])
    s.add_argument("--provider"); s.add_argument("--objective")
    s.add_argument("--robot", action="store_true", help="a scheduled job's run: the reaper retires it once a later run of the same job supersedes it (kit 29)")
    s.add_argument("--here", action="store_true", help="adopt the session worktree you are in (the desktop app's .claude/worktrees/<name>) instead of cutting a sibling; the repository it belongs to is the one meant, even if this script belongs to another")
    s.add_argument("--batch", metavar="ID", help="this lane is one of a batch's parallel lanes: cut it only above the "
                   "batch line (SESSION_BATCH_FREE_GB, default the warning line), else exit 75 so the batch queues it "
                   "(kit 41)")
    s = sub.add_parser("capacity", help="how many more lanes of this repository fit above the batch line now: exit 0 "
                       "when all --lanes fit, 75 when some must queue (kit 41; read-only)")
    s.add_argument("--lanes", type=int, default=1, help="lanes the batch wants to run at once (default 1)")
    s.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    s.add_argument("--here", action="store_true", help=other)
    s = sub.add_parser("status"); s.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    s.add_argument("--all", action="store_true", help="also list every task the remote holds as a claim or a backup, "
                   "other clones' included: holder, age, and whether it is ahead of the base branch (kit 40; read-only)")
    s.add_argument("--here", action="store_true", help=other)
    s = sub.add_parser("resume", help="continue another clone's unlanded task as a new attempt under this clone's own "
                       "name; the earlier attempt's refs and commits are never changed (kit 40)")
    s.add_argument("task", help="the task to continue: its backup ref, branch, task id or bare task name, as "
                   "`status --all` lists it")
    s.add_argument("--as", dest="as_name", metavar="NAME", help="the new attempt's task name (default: <task>-cont)")
    s.add_argument("--own", action="append", default=[], help="owned paths (default: the earlier attempt's)")
    s.add_argument("--provider"); s.add_argument("--objective")
    s.add_argument("--here", action="store_true", help=other)
    s = sub.add_parser("land"); s.add_argument("--title")
    s.add_argument("--pr", action="store_true", help="land through a pull request even where local landing is allowed")
    s.add_argument("--no-merge", action="store_true", help="open the PR and leave it for a human")
    s.add_argument("--local", action="store_true", help="land locally even for ask paths (skips the ruleset lookup)")
    s.add_argument("--kf11-verdict", choices=("yes", "no"), help="record the KF-11 reviewer's answer in the review "
                   "block (who and when, with --kf11-reviewer); no refuses the land (kit 34)")
    s.add_argument("--kf11-reviewer", metavar="NAME", help="the person who read the KF-11 diff (with --kf11-verdict)")
    s.add_argument("--owners-decided", metavar="TEXT", help="after a land refused on a conflict with someone else's "
                   "landing: who decided and what, in a few words. It goes into the task record, and this land then "
                   "stops at the conflict with the rebase open, to apply that decision (kit 40)")
    s.add_argument("--here", action="store_true", help=other)
    s = sub.add_parser("finish"); s.add_argument("--task")
    s.add_argument("--here", action="store_true", help=other)
    s = sub.add_parser("commit-guard", help="run by .agents/hooks/pre-commit: refuse a commit on the primary checkout, on a branch this worktree was not started on, or from a second session (kit 28)")
    s.add_argument("--here", action="store_true", help=other)
    s = sub.add_parser("retire", help="keep abandoned or superseded work on a pushed archive tag with a ledger row, then remove the worktree (kit 29)")
    s.add_argument("--reason", help="why, in a few words: it goes into the ledger row and the manifest")
    s.add_argument("--task", help="from the primary checkout: the session to retire")
    s.add_argument("--dry-run", action="store_true", help="check and report; change nothing")
    s.add_argument("--here", action="store_true", help=other)
    s = sub.add_parser("wip-backup", help="run by .agents/hooks/post-commit: push the branch tip to origin as refs/wip/<branch> (kit 29)")
    s.add_argument("--quiet", action="store_true", help="say nothing even when the push fails")
    s.add_argument("--here", action="store_true", help=other)
    a = p.parse_args()
    started, code = time.monotonic(), "uncaught exception"
    EVENT["verb"] = a.cmd
    verbs = {"start": cmd_start, "capacity": cmd_capacity, "status": cmd_status, "resume": cmd_resume,
             "land": cmd_land, "finish": cmd_finish,
             "commit-guard": cmd_commit_guard, "retire": cmd_retire, "wip-backup": cmd_wip_backup}
    try:
        code = verbs[a.cmd](a) or 0
        return code
    except SystemExit as e:
        code = e.code
        raise
    except Exception as e:
        if a.cmd != "commit-guard":
            raise
        code = 0  # the guard fails open: a bug in it must never wedge every commit in every repo
        EVENT["attrs"].update(decision="fail_open", error=type(e).__name__)
        print(f"session: commit-guard error ({type(e).__name__}); the commit goes on", file=sys.stderr)
        return 0
    finally:
        witness_report(a.cmd, code, started)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
